export const blogSections: BlogSection[] = [
  {
    id: "security",
    eyebrow: "Foundation, Deployed First",
    title: "The Badge That Opens Every Door",
    illustration: "vault.svg",
    diagram: null,
    airport: ["Before an airport can open a single gate, it needs a badge system. Every employee, every contractor, every vendor truck driver needs one credential that says exactly what doors they can open, and just as importantly, what doors they cannot. If that badge system is weak, nothing else in the airport matters, because a bad badge can walk anywhere.", "This project starts with exactly that. Before the runways, before the terminals, before anything else gets built, the badge system goes in first. Five separate keys get cut, one for the maintenance logs, one for the storage lockers, one for the vehicle fleet, one for anything sensitive, one for the alert system, so that losing one key never means losing all of them. Then a single master policy is written that says, no matter what any individual badge claims to allow, no badge may ever open the badge office itself, create new badges, or grant itself more access. That policy is not a suggestion. It is checked at every single door, every time, and it wins even if someone tries to argue their way past it.", "Every other part of the airport that gets built after this point, the terminals, the checkpoint, the tower, takes this badge system as a given rather than inventing its own. That is not an accident. It is the first and most important decision in the whole build."],
    technicalTitle: "The Security Foundation",
    technical: ["SecurityStack is the first thing that synthesizes in this project, and everything else waits on it. That ordering is not incidental. Every downstream stack, network_stack.py, inspection_stack.py, threetier_stack.py, privatelink_stack.py, takes SecurityStack's keys and permissions boundary in as constructor props rather than resolving them at deploy time through an SSM parameter lookup or a CloudFormation export. That distinction matters more than it looks like on paper. A prop is resolved when the CDK app is built, so the dependency graph is explicit and visible in code, and cdk synth fails loudly if something is missing rather than deploying successfully and then failing later at runtime because a cross-stack lookup came back empty. I built SecurityStack as the single source of truth for identity and encryption for exactly that reason. I wanted every other stack's IAM role and every other stack's bucket or log group encryption to trace back to one place I could reason about, instead of five stacks each quietly assuming a key or a boundary existed somewhere in the account.", "The stack provisions five separate customer managed KMS keys instead of one shared key, and that was a deliberate choice about blast radius, not an oversight. Each key comes out of a shared _make_key helper and is single purpose: LogsKey, aliased alias/lattice-lab-logs, for CloudWatch Logs encryption across the CloudTrail log group in governance_stack.py, the Suricata and nftables groups in observability_stack.py, and the Lambda log group in drift_remediation_stack.py. BucketsKey, alias/lattice-lab-buckets, for SSE-KMS on every S3 bucket in the project, including web, policy, access-logs, diagram, CloudTrail, and Config. EbsKey, alias/lattice-lab-ebs, for root volume encryption on every EC2 instance and launch template. SecretsKey, alias/lattice-lab-secrets, which is provisioned and exported as a prop even though nothing in the project currently stores a secret in it, since the RDS secret went away when threetier_stack.py moved to DynamoDB, kept around for whatever future stack needs one. And SnsKey, alias/lattice-lab-sns, for the governance-alerts topic in drift_remediation_stack.py. Every key has enable_key_rotation set to True, and every key's resource policy grants only two things: the account root, which is AWS's own recommended default so ordinary IAM policies and grants keep working, and the one AWS service principal that actually needs it, logs.amazonaws.com, s3.amazonaws.com, ec2.amazonaws.com, secretsmanager.amazonaws.com, or sns.amazonaws.com. The EC2 key policy goes one step further and adds a Bool condition, kms:GrantIsForAWSResource set to true, so the grant only fires for genuine AWS-managed EBS encryption rather than an arbitrary caller claiming to be EC2. Splitting the keys this way means a compromised grant on SnsKey has no path to SecretsKey. It costs almost nothing in operational overhead and it buys real containment.", "Sitting next to the CMKs is S3AccessLogsBucket, the one shared destination that every other bucket in the project, web, policy, lattice access logs, diagram, plus governance_stack.py's CloudTrail and Config buckets, points its own server_access_logs_bucket property at. It is deliberately encrypted with S3_MANAGED (SSE-S3) rather than the BucketsKey CMK, because it is receiving deliveries from the S3 Log Delivery Group, and CDK's bucket-policy-based log delivery path is the well-trodden one with SSE-S3; wiring SSE-KMS in here would only add key-policy and grant surface for a bucket that never holds anything more sensitive than access-pattern metadata. The bucket is still versioned, still BLOCK_ALL on public access, still enforce_ssl, and still set to RemovalPolicy.DESTROY with auto_delete_objects for the lab context. The one thing it does not have is its own server_access_logs_bucket, because a bucket cannot log to itself, and that gap is the single deliberate exception to the project's otherwise universal rule that every bucket gets access logging. I documented that exception directly against the cdk-nag AwsSolutions-S1 suppression on this bucket rather than leaving a silent gap for a reviewer to wonder about.", "The permissions boundary, PermissionsBoundary, deployed as an iam.ManagedPolicy named lattice-lab-permissions-boundary, is the part of this stack worth being precise about, because the term gets used loosely. A permissions boundary is not an allow list and it is not additive with a role's identity policy. It is a ceiling. When a boundary is attached to a role, that role's effective permissions are the intersection of what its identity policy allows and what the boundary allows, and an explicit Deny anywhere in either one wins outright regardless of what the identity policy says. Attaching a broad managed policy to a role bounded this way does not actually widen what the role can do past the boundary's own ceiling. That is exactly the shape of this policy: an AllowProjectOperations statement with a long, curated list of what this project's roles legitimately need, ec2:Describe*, ssm:*, ssmmessages:*, ec2messages:*, logs:*, ecs:*, vpc-lattice:*, scoped DynamoDB item actions, scoped S3 object actions, and more, sits alongside an AllowScopedPassRoleToSageMaker statement that permits iam:PassRole only when the condition key iam:PassedToService equals sagemaker.amazonaws.com, and an AllowProjectKmsUsage statement that scopes kms:Decrypt, kms:DescribeKey, kms:GenerateDataKey*, kms:Encrypt, and kms:ReEncrypt* to exactly the five CMK ARNs built earlier in the stack, deliberately leaving out key management actions. On top of all of that sits DenyPrivilegeEscalation, an explicit Deny on iam:CreateUser, iam:CreateRole, iam:CreatePolicy, iam:CreatePolicyVersion, iam:AttachUserPolicy, iam:AttachRolePolicy, iam:AttachGroupPolicy, iam:PutUserPolicy, iam:PutRolePolicy, iam:PutGroupPolicy, iam:UpdateAssumeRolePolicy, iam:PassRole, and the four actions that would let a role tamper with permissions boundaries themselves, iam:DeleteRolePermissionsBoundary, iam:DeleteUserPermissionsBoundary, iam:PutRolePermissionsBoundary, and iam:PutUserPermissionsBoundary, with a StringNotEquals condition on iam:PassedToService carving out the sagemaker.amazonaws.com case so the sagemaker_stack.py Lambdas can still legitimately pass SageMakerExecutionRole into CreateTrainingJob and similar calls. A second Deny, DenyAccountAndOrgActions, blocks organizations:*, account:*, kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion, and kms:DisableKey outright. No identity policy attached to a bounded role, however permissive, can talk its way past those two Deny statements, and that is precisely the property that makes a permissions boundary useful as a hard ceiling rather than just another policy to audit.", "The boundary only protects roles it is actually attached to, so the stack also produces SharedInstanceRole, named lattice-lab-shared-instance-role, trusted by ec2.amazonaws.com, carrying the AWS-managed AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy, and built with permissions_boundary set to self.permissions_boundary from the start, plus a matching SharedInstanceProfile wrapping it. This is the plain, SSM-managed identity any EC2 instance in the project can use when it does not need a bespoke role of its own. The bespoke roles that already existed elsewhere, firewall_role, libreswan_role, and the others with their own trust and least-privilege reasoning documented at each call site, were refactored in the same pass to also take permissions_boundary=security.permissions_boundary, so the boundary's coverage extends to every role this project's own code authors, without collapsing their distinct identities into one shared role. I was explicit in the module docstring about the scope decision behind that: I applied the boundary through per-role permissions_boundary props on the roles this project's code creates, not as a blanket iam.PermissionsBoundary.of(app) Aspect over the whole App or Stage, because an Aspect would also retroactively catch every IAM role CDK generates internally for its own plumbing, BucketDeployment's role, AwsCustomResource providers, log-retention custom resources, including things like lattice_stack.py's SelfSignedCertImport and LatticeManagedPrefixListLookup, whose exact runtime IAM needs are not enumerated anywhere in this project's code and are not verifiable from cdk synth alone. A boundary that turned out too narrow for one of those would only ever surface as an AccessDenied deep into a real deploy, so I scoped the boundary to get real, verifiable coverage on every role I actually designed rather than gambling an already-working deployment on an unverified blanket change.", "The last piece is IAM Access Analyzer, an accessanalyzer.CfnAnalyzer named lattice-lab-account-analyzer with type set to ACCOUNT. It runs at the account level and continuously evaluates resource-based policies, on the S3 buckets, the KMS keys, and anything else in the account with a resource policy, for any statement that grants access to a principal outside the account or its AWS Organization. It is a small piece of the stack on paper, a single CfnAnalyzer construct with two arguments, but it is the backstop that catches the failure mode none of the other controls in this stack are built to catch: a resource policy that, whether by a typo in a principal ARN or a wildcard added under deadline pressure, quietly opens something up to the internet or to an unrelated account. Everything else in SecurityStack, the five CMKs, the boundary, the shared role, is about constraining what principals inside this account can do. Access Analyzer is the one control watching for the door being left open from outside it, and putting it in the stack that deploys first and exports everything else as props meant that watch started running before a single other resource in the project existed."],
  },
  {
    id: "network",
    eyebrow: "The Ground Beneath Everything",
    title: "Four Runways, One Set of Rules",
    illustration: "checkpoint.svg",
    diagram: "network-inspection.svg",
    airport: ["An airport with four separate runway systems that each make up their own traffic rules is an airport waiting for a collision. This project builds four separate zones, one for the main application, one for a partner airline's counter, one dedicated entirely to inspection, and one standing in for a small regional airstrip connected by a private chartered route, and gives them one shared rulebook for how traffic moves between them.", "The rule is simple to say and strict in practice. Every zone's default route, no matter what it is trying to reach, passes through the inspection zone first. Not by convention, not by a note in a handbook that someone might forget to follow, but because the routing tables themselves have no other path. A plane cannot decide to skip the checkpoint because there is no taxiway that goes around it."],
    technicalTitle: "The Network Core",
    technical: ["The network core is four VPCs stitched together through a Transit Gateway, and the shape of each VPC's subnet tiers exists specifically to keep two jobs from colliding on the same route table. app-vpc (10.1.0.0/16, per the routing comments in the file) gets three tiers: Public, which holds the NAT Gateway and the internet gateway, TgwAttach, a /28 tier that hosts the Transit Gateway attachment ENIs and defaults 0.0.0.0/0 to that NAT Gateway, and Private, a /24 compute tier that defaults 0.0.0.0/0 to the Transit Gateway instead. inspection-vpc (10.0.0.0/16) needs a fourth tier because it carries two kinds of traffic that must never share a default route: Public (with its own NAT instance, more on that below), TgwAttach (/28, default route to the GWLB endpoint, wired later in inspection_stack.py), GwlbEndpoint (/28, default route back to the Transit Gateway), and Firewall (/24, default route to the NAT instance for the firewall fleet's own management traffic). provider-vpc (10.2.0.0/16) is the plain case, just Public and Private /24 tiers, with the Private tier's default route pointed at the Transit Gateway. onprem-vpc is the odd one out: it never gets a Transit Gateway VPC attachment at all. Its Public subnet holds the libreswan Customer Gateway instance and its Private subnet holds the placeholder broker, and the Site-to-Site VPN is the only path in or out, which is deliberate, the whole point is to behave like a real hybrid network reaching AWS over a VPN or Direct Connect rather than taking a VPC peering shortcut. One more detail worth flagging: app_az_count is pinned to at least two AZs (max(az_count, 2)) regardless of the MULTI_AZ flag, a holdover from when RDS's DBSubnetGroup required two AZ coverage, and the comment notes it was left in place after the data tier moved to DynamoDB because subnets themselves cost nothing and removing it risked destabilizing stacks that already reference those exact subnet names.", "The Transit Gateway itself (CfnTransitGateway, logical id Tgw, described in code as 'lattice-lab transit gateway') is built with default_route_table_association and default_route_table_propagation both set to disable, so nothing gets wired automatically, every association and every propagation in this stack is explicit. Two route tables do all the work: SpokeRouteTable (spoke_rt) and InspectionRouteTable (inspection_rt). AppVpcAttachment and ProviderVpcAttachment are both associated to spoke_rt and propagated into inspection_rt, and the VPN's auto-created attachment gets identical treatment later, associated to spoke_rt and propagated into inspection_rt. InspectionVpcAttachment, by contrast, is associated only to inspection_rt, and it carries options={'ApplianceModeSupport': 'enable'}, which the comment flags as mandatory once the GWLB target group on the other end also has appliance mode enabled, since both switches have to be on for flow symmetry. The routing logic is where the design intent shows up clearly. spoke_rt gets exactly one static route, 0.0.0.0/0 pointed at InspectionVpcAttachment, and nothing else, no propagated spoke CIDRs at all, which means app-vpc, provider-vpc, and the VPN cannot see each other's address space directly through this table. Every packet leaving a spoke, whether headed to the internet or to another spoke, lands in inspection-vpc first. inspection_rt is the mirror image: it collects the propagated routes from all three spoke attachments so return traffic can find its way home, plus two static routes added by hand. The first is 0.0.0.0/0 pointed at AppVpcAttachment, because app-vpc is the only spoke with a path to the internet, so this is what sends inspected north-south traffic back out through app-vpc's NAT Gateway once it clears the firewall. The second is the onprem CIDR pointed at the VPN's Transit Gateway attachment, added as a plain CfnTransitGatewayRoute because the VPN connection is created with static_routes_only=True, meaning there is no BGP session for that route to propagate from, so it has to be written explicitly or traffic to onprem would have nowhere to go once it reached inspection_rt.", "app-vpc's internet egress is a single managed NAT Gateway (CfnNatGateway, logical id AppNatGateway, backed by an Elastic IP called AppNatEip) placed by hand in the first Public subnet rather than through the Vpc construct's own nat_gateways parameter (all four VPCs set nat_gateways=0), specifically so the stack can control which subnet the gateway lands in and reference its id when wiring the TgwAttach tier's default route. That is SPEC.md Section 4's rule of one NAT Gateway total, made literal in code. inspection-vpc breaks that rule on purpose, but with an EC2 instance instead of a second managed NAT Gateway. InspectionNatInstance sits in inspection-vpc's own Public subnet with source_dest_check disabled and a security group, InspectionNatSg, that only accepts traffic from the inspection-vpc CIDR itself, and its user data does the router setup by hand: sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward=1, an iptables POSTROUTING MASQUERADE rule on whatever the default route's outbound interface turns out to be, and a small systemd unit, inspection-nat-rules.service, that restores those iptables rules from a saved file on every boot, since iptables rules do not otherwise survive a restart. The reason it cannot just ride app-vpc's shared NAT Gateway is worth spelling out in full. app-vpc's NAT sits behind an IGW ingress edge-association route table, built in inspection_stack.py, that redirects every packet returning to that NAT's Elastic IP through a GWLB endpoint for inspection. The firewall fleet's own outbound traffic, package updates and rule syncs, originates from inspection-vpc's own CIDR, which nftables classifies as untrust because it is neither the trust nor the dmz range, so that return traffic would loop back through the same GWLB redirect and hit the untrust to untrust path, which has no rule and defaults to deny. In effect, the firewall would be unable to update itself. A second, uninspected NAT keeps that recursion from ever happening, and using a plain EC2 instance instead of a second managed NAT Gateway keeps this narrow exception cheap relative to the project's one NAT Gateway cost intent.", "The simulated on-premises link stands in for a Direct Connect circuit, and it runs entirely on a Site-to-Site VPN with a libreswan instance playing the customer gateway device. LibreswanCgw is a plain EC2 instance in onprem-vpc's Public subnet with source_dest_check disabled, which the comment is careful to call out as the legitimate use of that setting here, a classic NAT-instance and router pattern, unlike the GWLB-fronted firewalls elsewhere in the stack. Its security group, LibreswanSg, allows UDP 500 (IKE) and UDP 4500 (IKE NAT-T) from any IPv4 address, which looks wide open until you remember that AWS's VPN service endpoints do not have fixed source IPs, so a real customer gateway appliance would need the same rule. On the AWS side, OnpremCustomerGateway is a CfnCustomerGateway with bgp_asn 65000 and its ip_address pointed at the libreswan instance's Elastic IP (LibreswanEip), and OnpremVpnConnection is created with static_routes_only=True and two tunnel options carrying pre-shared keys from _generate_psk, which prefixes a random 48 character hex string with the letter k so the value never starts with a zero, satisfying AWS's PSK format rule. Because a VPN attached to a Transit Gateway does not expose its auto-created TGW attachment id through CloudFormation's ordinary GetAtt (and because AWS::EC2::VPNConnectionRoute only works against a Virtual Private Gateway, not this setup), the stack looks the attachment id up at deploy time with a scoped AwsCustomResource, VpnTgwAttachmentLookup, calling describeTransitGatewayAttachments filtered on the VPN connection's resource id. That attachment id is what gets associated into spoke_rt, propagated into inspection_rt, and pointed at by the static onprem route described above.", "LibreswanEip attaches to the instance by instance_id, which means the instance has to exist before the EIP can attach, and that ordering means libreswan's own user data, baked in at instance create time, can never reference that EIP's eventual address without creating a circular CloudFormation dependency. So the boot script discovers everything for itself at runtime instead: it pulls its own public IP from the IMDSv2 metadata service, polls ec2:DescribeCustomerGateways for a gateway whose ip-address filter matches that IP, polls ec2:DescribeVpnConnections for the connection tied to that gateway, and then reads the two tunnels' OutsideIpAddress values out of VgwTelemetry, each stage retried up to ten times with a fifteen second sleep in between. From there it writes two libreswan connection files, aws-tunnel1.conf and aws-tunnel2.conf, both authby=secret and ikev2=no, with leftsubnet set to the onprem CIDR and rightsubnet 0.0.0.0/0, aes128-sha1 with modp1024 for both IKE and phase 2, an eight hour IKE lifetime, a one hour SA lifetime, and dead peer detection that restarts the tunnel after a thirty second timeout. The two pre-shared keys get written into /etc/ipsec.secrets and the file is chmod 600 before ipsec is enabled and restarted. Going the other direction, onprem-vpc's Private subnets, where the broker lives, get one summarized route back toward AWS: 10.0.0.0/14 pointed at the libreswan instance, which covers inspection-vpc (10.0.0.0/16), app-vpc (10.1.0.0/16), and provider-vpc (10.2.0.0/16) in a single line instead of three separate routes.", "The placeholder Kafka broker is intentionally minimal. PlaceholderBroker is an EC2 instance in onprem-vpc's Private subnet, and its user data installs nmap-ncat and runs a systemd unit, broker-placeholder.service, executing ncat -k -l 9092 with Restart=always, brought up immediately with systemctl enable --now. Its security group, BrokerSg, allows TCP 9092 in from 10.0.0.0/8, wide enough to cover the three AWS side /16s, inspection, app, and provider, that reach it over the tunnel without needing to enumerate each one by name. The comment above it says exactly what it is for: it gets swapped for a real Kafka broker in kafka_stack.py later, and until then it exists to prove that the path from an AWS-side subnet, through the Transit Gateway, into inspection_rt, across the VPN, and into onprem-vpc actually works end to end. That is also why the stack exports BrokerPrivateIp and BrokerInstanceId, alongside TransitGatewayId, VpnConnectionId, LibreswanPublicIp, and LibreswanInstanceId, as CfnOutputs meant for an SSM session and a simple nc verification step."],
  },
  {
    id: "inspection",
    eyebrow: "The Checkpoint",
    title: "The Building Every Flight Passes Through",
    illustration: null,
    diagram: null,
    airport: ["Inside the inspection zone sits the actual checkpoint, and it is built to never be a single point of failure. Multiple screening lanes run in parallel across two separate wings of the building, so if one wing has a problem, the other keeps working without anyone at the gate even noticing. A dispatcher at the front door decides which lane each flight goes to, and if a lane goes down entirely, the dispatcher stops sending traffic there and reroutes everyone to a working lane automatically.", "There is one detail that took real thought to get right. The inspectors staffing that checkpoint sometimes need to leave the building themselves, to pick up new rulebooks or restock supplies. If they used the same passenger checkpoint to do that, the inspection process would end up inspecting itself, which is both wasteful and, in a few edge cases, actually breaks things. So the inspectors have their own staff only exit, completely separate from the passenger flow, that never touches the checkpoint at all."],
    technicalTitle: "The Inspection Layer",
    technical: ["InspectionStack builds a Gateway Load Balancer named Gwlb (type gateway) that fronts a target group called FirewallTargetGroup, and the whole thing speaks GENEVE on port 6081, target_type instance. Health checking runs over TCP against port 8080, which is gwlbtun's confirmed health check port (GWLBTUN_HEALTHCHECK_PORT in the code, and it has to match the same constant baked into install-gwlbtun.sh on the box). Checks run every 10 seconds, and it takes 3 consecutive passes or failures (healthy_threshold_count and unhealthy_threshold_count, both set to 3) to flip an appliance's state. The target group also carries two explicit attributes, target_failover.on_deregistration and target_failover.on_unhealthy, both set to rebalance instead of AWS's default no_rebalance. That choice only matters because there are two appliances per AZ: rebalance means in-flight flows get moved onto the surviving healthy target immediately, instead of being black-holed until the Auto Scaling Group finishes replacing the dead instance.", "The firewall fleet itself is deliberately not one shared multi-AZ ASG. It's one FirewallAsg per Availability Zone, each pinned to min_capacity, max_capacity, and desired_capacity all equal to config.FIREWALL_APPLIANCES_PER_AZ, which is 2. The reasoning in the code is that a single multi-AZ ASG's built-in AZ rebalancing can transiently drift away from a strict per-AZ count during scaling events, and this design wants that count to hold exactly. Each ASG runs the same FirewallLaunchTemplate: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS pulled from Canonical's own SSM parameter path, IMDSv2 required, and a 20 GB gp3 root volume (not the 8 GB default) encrypted with security.ebs_key, sized up because building gwlbtun from source with Boost and CMake, plus pulling in Suricata's ET Open ruleset, needs real disk. Since GWLB target groups aren't one of the L2 AutoScalingGroup's attach_to_target_group helpers (those only cover ALB and NLB), the code drops to the L1 escape hatch, cfn_asg.target_group_arns, to wire each ASG straight into FirewallTargetGroup.", "Boot is where the nftables and Suricata story actually plays out. User data installs unzip, awscli, and nftables via apt-get, pulls down a software asset bundle from S3 (the install scripts, generate_nft.py, and pl-sync.sh, all zipped together because the bundle runs around 85KB, well past the 16KB user-data limit), and then sources install-gwlbtun.sh and install-suricata.sh from inside bash wrapper functions rather than executing them directly. The reason is that both scripts use return statements to abort early on a failed step, and return only behaves correctly inside a function or a sourced script, not a subprocess. After nftables and pl-sync's systemd units are in place, the boot script runs pl-sync.sh synchronously once before ever enabling pl-sync.timer, specifically so the ASG's health check, which has a 10 minute grace period to accommodate this whole install, never marks an instance healthy while it's still running with zero policy loaded. FirewallSg only opens two things, both scoped to the inspection VPC's own CIDR block: GENEVE on UDP 6081 from the GWLB, and TCP 8080 for the gwlbtun health check. The real enforcement boundary is the nftables policy itself, not this security group.", "Getting traffic into the firewall fleet requires two layers of GWLB Endpoints plus one route table trick that's easy to miss. GwlbEndpointService fronts the Gwlb with acceptance_required set to False, since this is a single-account lab and there's no cross-account PrivateLink handshake to gate. Because CfnVPCEndpointService doesn't expose a ServiceName attribute, the code builds the service name itself from the documented com.amazonaws.vpce.<region>.<service-id> format using attr_service_id. One GwlbEndpoint gets created per Availability Zone inside inspection-vpc's GwlbEndpoint subnets, and each TgwAttach subnet gets a 0.0.0.0/0 route pointed at the endpoint in the same AZ, matched explicitly by availability_zone rather than by list position. A second, separate set of endpoints, AppVpcGwlbEndpoint per subnet, gets created inside app-vpc itself, because a VPC endpoint can only serve as a route target for route tables that live in its own VPC. These app-vpc endpoints sit in the same public subnets as the NAT Gateway, since a GWLB Endpoint and a NAT Gateway are different resource types and happily coexist in one subnet.", "The redirect trick is the ingress route table, AppVpcIngressRouteTable, associated with app-vpc's internet gateway itself through CfnGatewayRouteTableAssociation, which is what makes it an edge-association (ingress) route table rather than an ordinary subnet route table. Without this piece, inbound reply packets for outbound-initiated sessions would land at the IGW and route straight to the NAT Gateway using the normal per-subnet route table, completely bypassing inspection on the return leg. Each route in that table, named IngressRedirect plus the subnet's construct id, matches the app-vpc public subnet's own CIDR block (that subnet only ever holds the NAT Gateway) and sends it to the matching app-vpc-local GWLB endpoint instead, since CfnNatGateway doesn't expose its private IP as a CDK attribute and a subnet CIDR match covers the same ground without an extra runtime lookup.", "Symmetry across all of this comes down to two things working together, and the module docstring is explicit that an earlier draft got this wrong. There is no target-group-level Appliance Mode toggle on a GWLB target group, that attribute simply doesn't exist. The actual Appliance Mode switch lives over in NetworkStack, on the inspection VPC's Transit Gateway attachment, set to appliance_mode_support enable. FirewallTargetGroup's job here is just to stay out of the way: it's left at its default 5-tuple stickiness, meaning no stickiness.* attributes are set on it at all, because 2-tuple or 3-tuple stickiness is documented as flatly incompatible with TGW Appliance Mode rather than an alternate way to express the same idea. Put together, the TGW attachment's Appliance Mode setting is what pins a given flow to one appliance for both directions, and the target group's default stickiness is what makes sure nothing at the GWLB layer second-guesses that decision by rehashing the flow onto a different one of the two appliances per AZ."],
  },
  {
    id: "application",
    eyebrow: "The Passenger Experience",
    title: "The Arrivals Hall and the Real Operation Behind It",
    illustration: "arrivals.svg",
    diagram: "application-path.svg",
    airport: ["Anyone can walk into an arrivals hall. It is public by design, there is a departure board on the wall, and there is no reason to check anyone's identity just to let them read it. But the actual work of running an airline, baggage routing, crew scheduling, gate assignments, happens in a back office that the public never sees and never should.", "This project draws that line carefully. The public hall is served straight from a private archive that nobody outside the airport can browse directly. For the handful of requests that need the real operation, there is exactly one hallway connecting the two, and that hallway only carries one kind of traffic. Nobody wanders in off the street and accidentally ends up in the back office.", "There is a second, entirely separate arrangement worth knowing about. A different partner runs their own small operation elsewhere on the property, and this project's application needs to reach exactly one counter at that partner's office. Rather than merging the two properties together, which would mean either side could eventually wander into the other's territory, a single narrow connection gets built that only goes one direction. The partner's office has no path back into this project's territory at all, even if it wanted one."],
    technicalTitle: "The Application Path",
    technical: ["The three tier stack, defined in threetier_stack.py, is the classic web, app, and data separation, built so the web tier can never touch the database directly and only the app tier holds that permission. CloudFront sits in front of everything as the single public entry point, with two behaviors defined on the WebDistribution: a default behavior that serves static assets out of a private S3 bucket, and an /api/* behavior that forwards to the internal application load balancer. Nothing in the app tier is internet facing on its own. The ALB, AppAlb, is created with internet_facing=False and lives in the app VPC's Private subnets, so the only way to reach it is through CloudFront's VPC origin, not through any public listener someone could hit directly.", "The web tier used to be a placeholder page, but it isn't anymore. app/frontend-next is a real Next.js and Tailwind application built with output: 'export' in its next.config.js, and the pipeline's Synth step runs that build before cdk synth ever executes, so by the time threetier_stack.py calls s3_deploy.Source.asset() against app/frontend-next/out, there are real static files sitting there to upload. The WebBucket itself stays locked down: versioned, KMS encrypted with security.buckets_key, block_public_access set to BLOCK_ALL, enforce_ssl on, and reachable only through an Origin Access Control on the S3BucketOrigin that CloudFront uses. Because Next.js's static export writes one directory per route (a post like blog/hybrid-cloud-airport-story ends up as blog/hybrid-cloud-airport-story/index.html), and a private, OAC backed bucket doesn't auto append index.html to subdirectory requests the way an S3 website endpoint would, the stack adds a small CloudFront Function, NextStaticUrlRewrite, running on the JS_2_0 runtime at the default behavior's viewer request event, which rewrites any URI ending in a slash, or containing no dot at all, to its index.html file right at the edge. That keeps the whole rewrite free tier eligible with no Lambda@Edge involved.", "The /api/* behavior is where CloudFront's VPC origin feature does the real work: origins.VpcOrigin.with_application_load_balancer(self.alb, http_port=80) lets CloudFront reach directly into the VPC and hit the ALB's port 80 listener, with an origin request policy of ALL_VIEWER_EXCEPT_HOST_HEADER and caching disabled, so there is no need for a public facing load balancer at all. That listener, AppListener, forwards to AppTargetGroup on port 8080, target_type IP rather than INSTANCE, because Fargate's awsvpc networking mode has no host instance to register a target by id against. The target group's health check hits /api/health with a healthy_threshold_count of 2 and an unhealthy_threshold_count of 3. Behind that target group sits AppService, an ECS Fargate service on the smallest task size (256 CPU units, 512 MiB of memory) built from app/backend's Dockerfile through ecs.ContainerImage.from_asset(), running at a desired_count of 2 when MULTI_AZ is on and 1 otherwise, with enable_execute_command turned on for ECS Exec access and a deployment circuit breaker set to roll back automatically on failure.", "The data tier is a single DynamoDB table named latticelab, with a plain string partition key called id, on demand billing, an AWS managed KMS key, and point in time recovery turned on. Access to it isn't gated by a security group at all, since DynamoDB has no listening port of its own to control; instead the stack calls grant_read_write_data() straight onto the app task's IAM role, which is the right control surface for a managed API endpoint like this one rather than a network level one. Getting Fargate tasks to even start inside the app VPC required its own set of VPC endpoints: ECR, ECR Docker, and CloudWatch Logs as interface endpoints, plus S3 and DynamoDB as gateway endpoints, after discovering the hard way that routing the ECR auth handshake out through the NAT gateway and the inspection path wasn't fast or reliable enough (the task failed with an i/o timeout dialing port 443 during registry auth). One more resource rounds out this stack: LatticeInstanceTargetHost, a small dedicated EC2 instance running the same backend code as a systemd service. It exists purely because VPC Lattice's INSTANCE type target groups need a real EC2 instance id to register against, something a Fargate task can never provide, so this one host preserves that demo path without putting the actual app tier workload back on EC2.", "The PrivateLink pattern in privatelink_stack.py is a deliberately separate exercise from the three tier app, built to prove a one way connection between two VPCs that never peer. A minimal stdlib HTTP server runs on Fargate in the provider VPC, on ProviderCluster and ProviderTaskDefinition at the same 256 CPU, 512 MiB task size, listening on port 8080, behind an internal Network Load Balancer named ProviderNlb whose NetworkTargetGroup uses IP targets on TCP port 8080. That NLB gets wrapped in a CfnVPCEndpointService with acceptance_required set to False, which fits a single account lab rather than a real cross account offering that would need a manual accept step. On the consumer side, app_vpc creates an InterfaceVpcEndpoint called ProviderInterfaceEndpoint against that service's generated name, with private_dns_enabled left off since a friendly DNS name would need domain ownership verification that doesn't make sense for a lab; consumers resolve the provider through the endpoint's own generated DNS name instead, which the stack exposes as a CfnOutput.", "What makes this one way rather than a peering relationship is that PrivateLink projects the provider's NLB into the consumer's VPC as an ENI, and nothing more. There is no VPC peering connection anywhere in this pair of stacks, no route table entry for the provider VPC's CIDR inside app-vpc, and no route table entry for app-vpc's CIDR inside the provider VPC. The consumer can reach the provider through that ENI, but the provider has no corresponding path back into the consumer's VPC at all. Because there is no routing between the two CIDR blocks, they could overlap completely without any conflict, even though in this project they happen not to, sitting at 10.1.0.0/16 and 10.2.0.0/16. The stack also has to account for availability zones on the consumer side: a VPC Endpoint Service only supports the AZs its backing NLB actually has nodes in, so the interface endpoint's subnet selection is restricted to just the AZs the provider's NLB is deployed in, rather than all of app-vpc's Private subnets, since the two VPCs' AZ counts are not forced to match each other."],
  },
  {
    id: "lattice",
    eyebrow: "The Centerpiece",
    title: "The Private Walkways Between Specific Gates",
    illustration: "concierge.svg",
    diagram: "lattice.svg",
    airport: ["Most of an airport's traffic moves through the main concourse, but there is a smaller, more interesting system running underneath it: private walkways that connect specific gates directly to each other, skipping the general concourse entirely. A badge check happens at every single doorway along that walkway, every time, with no exceptions for someone who was already checked five minutes earlier.", "What makes this system worth building at all is how smart it is about routing. Show one kind of boarding pass and you go to one gate. Show a different marker, a canary wristband for instance, and you get quietly routed to a gate the airport is testing, invisible to everyone but the operators running the test. Ninety percent of travelers go the normal way, ten percent get routed through the new gate, live, with real passengers, and nobody outside the airport notices anything happening.", "The walkway system connects to three genuinely different kinds of destinations at once, a staffed counter, an automated kiosk, and an existing gate that was already running its own separate operation before this walkway system existed. All three get treated the same way by the walkway's own rules, which is exactly the point. It also opens its own separate door straight out to the regional airstrip mentioned earlier, a second and completely independent way to reach that same outside destination, distinct from the chartered flight route the network layer uses."],
    technicalTitle: "The VPC Lattice Service Mesh",
    technical: ["Everything in this stack hangs off a single CfnServiceNetwork named lattice-lab, provisioned with auth_type set to AWS_IAM so that every downstream invocation has to carry a signed request rather than relying on network location alone. The first service attached to it, CfnService lattice-lab-app, fronts the three tier app's compute through an INSTANCE type target group called lattice-lab-instance-tg. That target group listens on port 8080 (the same APP_TIER_PORT constant the three tier stack uses), runs an HTTP health check against /api/health, and calls a target healthy after 2 consecutive successes and unhealthy after 3 consecutive failures. Because Fargate tasks cannot register as INSTANCE type Lattice targets, the target here is a dedicated EC2 instance (threetier.lattice_instance_target_host) that exists purely to keep this demo real instead of mocked. An HTTP listener on port 80 forwards to that target group at weight 100, and that forward block is the service's default rule. VPC Lattice has no separate default CfnRule resource, the default_action on the listener is the rule. A CfnServiceNetworkServiceAssociation attaches the service to the network, and a CfnServiceNetworkVpcAssociation attaches app_vpc to the network with a purpose built security group, LatticeVpcAssociationSg, guarding the association.", "On top of that default rule sit three progressively more interesting routing rules, all attached to the same http_listener with ascending priority values so they evaluate in a defined order. Priority 10, named path-v1, matches any request whose path starts with the prefix /v1/ (case insensitive) and forwards it at weight 100 to a second target group, lattice-lab-ip-tg, which is an IP type target group pointed at the exact same backend instance but addressed by its private IP instead of its instance ID, a clean way to prove out the IP target type without spinning up new infrastructure. Priority 20, named header-canary, matches on the header x-canary with an exact value of true and forwards to lattice-lab-lambda-tg, a LAMBDA type target group backed by a Python 3.13 inline function (CanaryLambda) that returns a small JSON payload announcing itself as the Lambda target group. Priority 30, named weighted-canary, matches the /canary/ prefix and splits traffic 90/10 across two target groups in a single WeightedTargetGroupProperty list, sending 90 percent to the original instance target group and 10 percent to lattice-lab-alb-tg, an ALB type target group that points straight at the three tier stack's application load balancer ARN. Between the path match, the header match, and the weighted split, this one listener demonstrates all three of Lattice's rule based routing patterns off a shared backend footprint.", "L4c layers HTTPS and TLS passthrough onto the same service network. Since there is no real domain in this lab to validate a public ACM certificate against, and ACM Private CA runs roughly 400 dollars a month just to exist, the stack generates its own self-signed certificate with openssl at synth time (RSA 2048, 3650 day validity, CN of lattice-lab.internal.example) and imports it into ACM through an AwsCustomResource calling ACM's importCertificate API directly, since ImportCertificate has no native CloudFormation resource type. The certificate's SAN list covers both lattice-lab.internal.example and tls.lattice-lab.internal.example so the one cert can satisfy both services Lattice validates against. certificate_arn and custom_domain_name are set as direct L1 property assignments on the CfnService object rather than constructor arguments, because in the Lattice CFN model those fields belong to the service, not the listener. An HttpsListener on port 443 forwards to the same instance target group used by the HTTP listener. A second service, TlsPassthroughService (lattice-lab-tls), carries its own custom domain (tls.lattice-lab.internal.example is required because Lattice rejects a TLS_PASSTHROUGH listener on a service without a custom domain, and reusing the main service's domain across two services would collide) and its own TLS_PASSTHROUGH listener on port 443, forwarding raw encrypted bytes to lattice-lab-tls-tg, an INSTANCE type target group on port 443 over TCP with health checks disabled, since Lattice never terminates TLS here and cannot health check through it. This path is structurally complete but not yet end to end verified, since the app tier instance only listens on plain HTTP 8080 today and has no TLS listener of its own to receive the passed through traffic.", "L4d is the zero trust layer sitting underneath all of that routing. A CfnAuthPolicy is attached to the service network's ARN and a second, identically shaped one to the service's ARN, both statements allowing vpc-lattice-svcs:Invoke from any principal but gated by a condition requiring aws:SourceVpc to equal app_vpc's VPC ID, so IAM identity alone is not enough, the request also has to originate from the expected VPC. As defense in depth alongside that policy, the stack looks up the AWS managed prefix list for VPC Lattice itself (com.amazonaws.<region>.vpc-lattice, resolved through an AwsCustomResource call to describeManagedPrefixLists) and adds an ingress rule to LatticeVpcAssociationSg that only allows traffic from that prefix list, on all ports, into the VPC association's security group.", "L4e is the hybrid path, and it is where the stack reaches outside AWS entirely. CfnResourceGateway OnpremResourceGateway sits in app_vpc's private subnets behind ResourceGatewaySg, and it exists to reach an on-premises broker over TGW and VPN. CfnResourceConfiguration OnpremBrokerResourceConfig is a SINGLE type, TCP protocol resource configuration scoped to port 9092 (a Kafka style broker port) with its resource_configuration_definition pointing at network.broker's private IP address, and a CfnServiceNetworkResourceAssociation wires that resource configuration into the same lattice-lab service network everything else lives on, so on-prem resources become addressable through Lattice right alongside the AWS native services. The stack also stands up a second, independent access pattern in provider_vpc: an ec2.CfnVPCEndpoint of type ServiceNetwork, pointed at the service network's ARN, guarded by ServiceNetworkEndpointSg whose only ingress rule permits traffic from provider_vpc's own CIDR block. Because provider_vpc is deliberately never given a full CfnServiceNetworkVpcAssociation, this PrivateLink powered endpoint is the demonstration of the lighter weight alternative, a VPC that wants to reach the network's services without formally joining it.", "The observability and administrative layer, L4f, closes the stack out. ServiceNetworkAccessLogs is a CloudWatch Logs group with one week retention, encrypted with security.logs_key, and ServiceAccessLogsBucket is a versioned, KMS encrypted S3 bucket (using security.buckets_key with bucket key enabled), fully blocking public access, enforcing SSL, and shipping its own server access logs to security.s3_access_logs_bucket under the prefix lattice-access-logs-bucket/. The bucket's resource policy grants vpc-lattice.amazonaws.com PutObject scoped by an aws:SourceAccount condition, and separately the KMS key grants encrypt and decrypt to that same service principal, since SSE-KMS delivery needs both the S3 side and the KMS side authorized independently. Two CfnAccessLogSubscription resources then wire the actual delivery: one on the service network's ARN delivering to the log group, one on the service's ARN delivering to the S3 bucket, so network level and service level access logs land in different destinations. A second, independently routable Lambda backed service, DualStackService (lattice-lab-dualstack), fans the network out further with its own target group, listener, and association, though the original intent of using it to demonstrate IPv6 dual stack via ip_address_type on the target group config turned out to be a dead end. VPC Lattice's API rejects that property outright for LAMBDA type target groups, since ip_address_type is only honored on IP type target groups, which would need real IPv6 enabled subnets this lab intentionally does not provision. Finally, an AWS RAM cross account share is wired in behind a config.ENABLE_RAM_SHARE flag, a CfnResourceShare named lattice-lab-service-network-share that shares the service network's ARN with config.SECOND_ACCOUNT_ID and sets allow_external_principals to False. When that flag is off, which is the default without a second account ID configured, the stack instead emits a CfnOutput spelling out exactly what the share would have created, so the cross account design is documented and reviewable even when it is not actually deployed."],
  },
  {
    id: "governance",
    eyebrow: "Watching Everything, Fixing the Small Things",
    title: "The Security Operations Center",
    illustration: "soc.svg",
    diagram: "security-governance.svg",
    airport: ["Every well run airport has a room full of screens that nobody outside security ever sees. Cameras record everything, not so someone can watch live all day, but so that if something goes wrong at two in the morning, there is a complete record of exactly what happened. A compliance inspector walks the terminals on a fixed schedule, checking that fire doors are not propped open and that badges have not been left in an unlocked drawer. A threat desk watches for patterns that look like the start of trouble, not just individual rule breaks.", "When something small and well understood goes wrong, a door left unlocked by mistake, an automatic response team fixes that one specific door immediately rather than paging the airport director to handle it personally. That team never runs a broad sweep through the whole property. It only ever touches the one thing the alert actually named."],
    technicalTitle: "Governance, Drift Remediation, and Auto Heal",
    technical: ["The landing zone runs three independent control loops instead of one unified one, and that separation is a deliberate design choice, not an accident of how the code grew. GovernanceStack is a pure detection plane: CloudTrail, AWS Config, Security Hub, and GuardDuty, none of which have the ability to change anything, they only observe and score. DriftRemediationStack is a security control aimed at a specific threat model, a human making a manual change outside the CI/CD pipeline, and it is the one place in the whole project where a Lambda actually holds delete permissions triggered off a live API call. AutoHealStack is an operations control aimed at a completely different problem, three named, well understood infrastructure failure modes that have nothing to do with who made a change and everything to do with keeping already approved infrastructure running. Collapsing these into a single mega-remediator would mean one execution role and one code path carrying both the logic that decides whether to delete a stranger's EC2 instance and the logic that reboots a known host on a failed status check, two very different trust boundaries and blast radii that are much easier to reason about, test, and suppress cdk-nag findings against when they stay apart.", "GovernanceStack wires up CloudTrail as a multi-region trail named lattice-lab-trail, with include_global_service_events, enable_file_validation, and management_events set to ReadWriteType.ALL, delivering to both a CMK-encrypted, versioned S3 bucket and a CloudWatch log group (CloudTrailLogGroup, one month retention) that is what EventBridge actually reads in practice. Getting that CloudTrail bucket working required an extra explicit KMS grant, security.buckets_key.grant_encrypt_decrypt(iam.ServicePrincipal(\"cloudtrail.amazonaws.com\")), because the Trail L2 construct wires the bucket policy automatically but not the CMK's own resource policy, which surfaced as a live deploy failure before being caught. AWS Config runs a single recorder named lattice-lab-recorder with all_supported and include_global_resource_types both true, paired with a delivery channel named lattice-lab-delivery-channel, and the stack deliberately declares no CDK dependency between the two because both directions were tried and both failed live (the recorder deadlocks waiting on a channel that never starts, or the channel throws NoAvailableConfigurationRecorderException), so each resource's own PUT call is left to succeed on its own per the documented fix in aws/aws-cdk#3492. On top of the recorder sit eight managed Config rules: required tags (keyed on Project and Layer), S3 bucket versioning, S3 server side encryption, EBS encrypted volumes, CloudTrail enabled, VPC flow logs enabled, restricted SSH ingress, and no IAM policy statements with admin access. Security Hub is enabled with enable_default_standards set to false so the stack can pin exactly two standards on purpose, AWS Foundational Security Best Practices v1.0.0 and CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0 (the code notes that v1.2.0 no longer exists as a subscribable standard, confirmed against the live Security Hub API), and GuardDuty runs as a single CfnDetector enabled unconditionally because its cost at lab scale is negligible. Because none of Config, Security Hub, or GuardDuty publish a native CloudWatch findings-count metric, a small Lambda named governance-metrics-publisher polls all three every 15 minutes and writes into a custom namespace, LatticeLab/Governance, purely so the observability dashboard has something to graph.", "DriftRemediationStack answers a narrower and more dangerous question: what happens within seconds of someone making a change by hand instead of through the pipeline. An EventBridge rule, ManualMutationRule, watches CloudTrail's AWS API Call via CloudTrail management events for eventName prefixes Create, Run, Put, Modify, Delete, Attach, and Authorize, filtered to exclude anything whose userIdentity.arn starts with the CDK bootstrap's shared execution role, cdk-hnb659fds-cfn-exec-role-{account}-{region}, which every pipeline deploy and every manual cdk deploy under the deloitte-admin profile authenticates through. Because a Lambda with delete permissions triggered off a raw API call is inherently risky, the design stacks four independent gates on top of that first EventBridge filter: the handler re-derives the same execution role exclusion itself, it does a live IAM tag lookup for BreakGlass=true on the calling principal, it does a live tag lookup for ManagedBy=cdk on the resource the event actually touched, it only recognizes a small explicit allow list of eventSource and eventName pairs with no generic best effort delete path, and config.DRY_RUN (false by default) can flip the whole thing to alert only. The Lambda itself, drift-remediator, runs inside app_vpc's Private subnets behind a security group named RemediatorSg with outbound only egress, and its IAM role is split into two statements, ReadForGating (describe and list-tags calls across ec2, s3, iam, dynamodb, sns, lambda, and logs) and ScopedRemediation (an explicit, non-wildcard list of delete actions: ec2 TerminateInstances, DeleteSecurityGroup, DeleteVolume, s3 DeleteBucket, iam DeleteRole, dynamodb DeleteTable, sns DeleteTopic, lambda DeleteFunction, logs DeleteLogGroup). Every alert, whether the Lambda takes action or not, goes to a CMK-encrypted SNS topic named governance-alerts with an email subscription to config.REMEDIATION_EMAIL. The net effect the module docstring calls out directly is that this mechanism can only ever delete one specific, newly created, untagged resource made by a non-break-glass principal outside the pipeline, never a sweep, never anything pipeline-managed.", "AutoHealStack is not a security control at all, it is a resilience loop for three named failure modes, each mapped to exactly one resource the stack already holds a reference to. The first is vpn-tunnel: a CloudWatch alarm named VpnTunnelDownAlarm watches the AWS/VPN TunnelState metric on network_stack's Site-to-Site VPN connection, threshold 1, comparison LESS_THAN_THRESHOLD, two evaluation periods, missing data treated as breaching, and on alarm it dispatches an SSM Run Command using the AWS-RunShellScript document against the Libreswan instance to restart ipsec and force IKE renegotiation. The second is lattice-target: LatticeTargetUnhealthyAlarm watches AWS/EC2 StatusCheckFailed on threetier_stack's Lattice INSTANCE-type target host, threshold 1, GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO_THRESHOLD, two evaluation periods, missing data treated as not breaching, and remediation is a plain ec2:RebootInstances call scoped to that one instance ARN. The third, bedrock-throttle, only gets wired when agentic_ai is present (config.ENABLE_AI on): one alarm per persona on the orchestrator Lambda's error metric, threshold of 3 errors, one evaluation period, and it is intentionally alert only, since a Bedrock TPS quota problem needs a support ticket rather than an infrastructure action. All three alarms feed a Step Functions state machine named auto-heal that runs NotifyBefore (an SnsPublish task to drift_remediation.alerts_topic with subject auto-heal: remediation starting), then RemediateTask (a LambdaInvoke against a function named auto-heal-remediate that dispatches on a remediation_type field), then NotifyAfter (SnsPublish with subject auto-heal: remediation complete), with logging level set to ALL and X-Ray tracing enabled on the state machine. Every alarm also fires an SnsAction straight to the same governance-alerts topic independent of the Step Functions path, so a human gets the identical signal the automation is acting on rather than having to trust the automation silently.", "Read side by side, the three loops map cleanly onto three different questions the landing zone needs answered, and none of them substitute for each other. Governance answers is the account compliant right now, on a recurring evaluation cycle, with zero mutate permissions anywhere in that stack. Drift remediation answers did someone just do something outside the pipeline, and it earns its delete permissions only after passing four separate, independently falsifiable gates because that is the one loop where the blast radius of a mistake is a human's infrastructure getting deleted. Auto heal answers is a known, named piece of infrastructure currently broken in one of three specific ways, and it never touches anything it doesn't already hold a direct construct reference to, whether that's network.libreswan.instance_id or threetier.lattice_instance_target_host.instance_id. Keeping them as three stacks also means three independently testable, independently disableable surfaces: DRY_RUN only affects drift remediation, the Config managed rules run on their own schedule regardless of whether the remediation Lambda is even deployed, and auto heal's alarms can be tuned or unwired per failure mode without touching the CloudTrail-driven security path at all. That separation is the whole point. A single combined Lambda watching both unauthorized human changes and known infrastructure failures would have to reason about two unrelated trust boundaries in one place, and the cdk-nag suppressions attached to each stack, each with its own written justification for IAM4, IAM5, and the Step Functions specific SF1 and SF2 findings, are the clearest evidence that each mechanism was scoped and audited on its own terms rather than inherited from a shared template."],
  },
  {
    id: "observability",
    eyebrow: "One Wall, Every Terminal",
    title: "The Control Room",
    illustration: "control-room.svg",
    diagram: null,
    airport: ["Somewhere in the airport there is a control room with a wall of screens, and every terminal, every checkpoint lane, every gate feeds a live signal into that same wall rather than keeping its own private monitor somewhere nobody looks at. When a new wing gets added to the airport, its status screen gets bolted onto the same wall instead of getting its own separate room built for it.", "That sounds like a small detail, but it changes how the airport actually gets operated day to day. One person can glance at one wall and know whether the checkpoint lanes are backed up, whether the private walkways are healthy, whether the construction crew's last renovation went smoothly, all without walking the property."],
    technicalTitle: "Observability and Supporting Stacks",
    technical: ["ObservabilityStack builds a single CloudWatch dashboard named lattice-lab (the CfnDashboard construct id is just Dashboard, dashboard_name is the string lattice-lab), and everything downstream treats that dashboard as a shared mutable object rather than a sealed artifact. The stack opens with a TextWidget reading lattice-lab -- interview demo health check at full 24-column width, then appends section after section as it wires in the other stacks it depends on: NetworkStack, InspectionStack, PrivateLinkStack, ThreeTierStack, LatticeStack, GovernanceStack, DriftRemediationStack, and SecurityStack are all passed in as keyword arguments and read directly for their metrics and ARNs. The interesting design choice is that self.dashboard is exposed as a plain CDK construct reference, not something scoped only to this file's own code, so optional layers that deploy later (Cloud WAN, Agentic AI, SageMaker, auto-heal) reach back into this same dashboard object and call add_widgets on it from their own stack files, auto_heal_stack.py being the concrete example already doing this. That ordering matters enough that the module docstring calls it out explicitly: ObservabilityStack now has to deploy after GovernanceStack and DriftRemediationStack in stage.py, a reversal from the original build, because its dashboard graphs governance_stack.py's custom metrics and drift_remediation_stack.py's Lambda directly and those resources need to exist first.", "Before any widgets get added, the stack creates two CloudWatch log groups scoped to the inspection fleet only, SuricataEveLogGroup at /inspection-vpc/suricata-eve and NftablesPlLogGroup at /inspection-vpc/nft-pl, both with one week retention (logs.RetentionDays.ONE_WEEK), both encrypted with security.logs_key, and both set to RemovalPolicy.DESTROY so a lab teardown actually removes them. The module docstring is candid about a known gap here: these two groups exist with the right names and retention to match docs/inspection-architecture-reference.md section 5.3, but inspection_stack.py's launch template user data does not yet install or configure the CloudWatch Agent to actually ship /var/log/... into them, so they currently exist and cost nothing while sitting empty, and nothing populates them until that agent configuration lands as a follow up. Lattice's own service network and service access log destinations are deliberately not duplicated in this stack either, since those already live in lattice_stack.py as a CloudWatch Logs group plus an S3 bucket, created there because that stack needed them immediately to wire a CfnAccessLogSubscription.", "The dashboard body itself is organized into labeled sections built from cw.GraphWidget and cw.LogQueryWidget instances. The first block, carried over from the original build, graphs GWLB firewall fleet health (HealthyHostCount and UnHealthyHostCount on the AWS/GatewayELB namespace) next to Lattice service traffic (TotalRequestCount and HTTPCode_5XX_Count on AWS/VpcLattice, keyed to the Service dimension value lattice-lab-app, the service's configured Name rather than a generated id or ARN), followed by an app tier ALB widget pulling threetier.alb.metrics.request_count() and the app target group's healthy host count and target response time, and a DynamoDB widget graphing consumed read and write capacity units against throttled requests. Both the GWLB and the later NLB widgets rely on a small helper, _elbv2_dimension_value, which pulls the sixth colon-separated field out of an ELBv2 ARN using cdk.Fn.select(5, cdk.Fn.split(':', arn)), a single extraction confirmed to work identically across AWS/GatewayELB, AWS/NetworkELB, and AWS/ApplicationELB dimension formats. A Network section follows with its own text header widget, graphing Site-to-Site VPN tunnel state (AWS/VPN TunnelState keyed to network.vpn_connection's VpnId, labeled 0=DOWN, 1=UP), Transit Gateway BytesIn and BytesOut against PacketDropCountBlackhole and PacketDropCountNoRoute, and the managed App NAT Gateway's BytesOutToDestination against PacketsDropCount and ErrorPortAllocation on AWS/NATGateway. A separate widget then graphs the self-managed inspection NAT instance using AWS/EC2 CPUUtilization and StatusCheckFailed instead, since that path runs as a plain EC2 instance and never gets AWS/NATGateway metrics the way the managed gateway does, a distinction the code comments call out directly. A PrivateLink section graphs the provider NLB's healthy and unhealthy host counts alongside ActiveFlowCount and NewFlowCount on AWS/NetworkELB.", "Governance and Drift Remediation get their own sections toward the bottom of the dashboard. Because Config, Security Hub, and GuardDuty have no native findings-over-time metric, the Governance widgets read from a custom namespace, LatticeLab/Governance, graphing NonCompliantConfigRules, ActiveFindingsCritical, ActiveFindingsHigh, ActiveFindingsMedium, ActiveFindingsLow, and GuardDutyFindingsTotal, each on a 15 minute period with a Maximum statistic, populated by governance_stack.py's own metrics publisher Lambda. Next to that sits a LogQueryWidget running a CloudWatch Logs Insights query against governance.trail_log_group (fields @timestamp, eventName, then stats count(*) as events by bin(5m)) because CloudTrail itself doesn't expose a native metric for management event volume. The Drift Remediation section graphs drift_remediation.remediator_fn's invocation and error counts side by side with the governance-alerts SNS topic's number of messages published. Three layers are deliberately left off the whole dashboard, and the docstring says so plainly: KafkaStack is still a SPEC.md TODO skeleton with nothing running to graph, DiagramStack is a static CloudFront and S3 site with no runtime metrics worth a widget, and Region2Stack is an SSM only test instance whose existence is already covered by CloudFormation's own stack status.", "ResourceGroupsStack is a much smaller, purely tag-based construct. It builds one umbrella CfnGroup named lattice-lab-all using resource_query type TAG_FILTERS_1_0 against resource_type_filters AWS::AllSupported and a single tag filter on Project equal to config.PROJECT_TAG, then loops over config.ALL_LAYERS to build one additional group per layer, each named lattice-lab-{layer}, each construct id the capitalized layer name suffixed with LayerGroup, and each filtered on both the Project tag and a Layer tag matching that specific layer value. Every group gets a CfnOutput exposing its name, UmbrellaGroupName for the umbrella group and a per-layer output named after the capitalized layer. The stack is intentionally left untagged with its own Layer value, on the reasoning stated directly in the code, that a tag-based query group which is itself part of the tag set it queries would be confusing to reason about, and there's nothing else in this stack that would benefit from being grouped that way anyway.", "DiagramStack hosts the architecture reference site that this whole diagram-site directory produces, and it leans entirely on a private origin pattern rather than public website hosting. The S3 bucket, construct id DiagramBucket, blocks all public access, is versioned, encrypted with KMS via security.buckets_key with bucket_key_enabled turned on, enforces SSL, and ships its server access logs to security.s3_access_logs_bucket under the prefix diagram-bucket/, with removal_policy DESTROY and auto_delete_objects true so a lab teardown actually empties it. Static website hosting mode is deliberately left off, since that mode serves over plain HTTP and doesn't support Origin Access Control at all. In front of the bucket sits a CloudFront Distribution, construct id DiagramDistribution, using origins.S3BucketOrigin.with_origin_access_control (the modern OAC pattern rather than a legacy OAI), default_root_object index.html, minimum_protocol_version TLS_V1_2_2021, viewer_protocol_policy REDIRECT_TO_HTTPS, and allowed_methods restricted to ALLOW_GET_HEAD. A ResponseHeadersPolicy named lattice-lab-diagram-{region} attaches a content security policy of default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; base-uri 'none', a policy that's actually enforceable because the generated page carries zero script tags and makes no external requests of any kind, alongside nosniff content type options, DENY frame options, a strict-origin-when-cross-origin referrer policy, and HSTS with a 365 day max age and include_subdomains enabled. A BucketDeployment named DiagramDeployment uploads the diagram-site directory (excluding generate_diagram.py, the script that produces the page's computed, grid-snapped SVG coordinates), invalidates /index.html on the distribution after each deploy, and sets cache-control to public, max-age=300, must-revalidate, so the short TTL plus the explicit invalidation is what actually forces freshness rather than any cache-busting file naming scheme. There's no custom domain and no WAF or signed URL restriction, so the output DiagramUrl resolves to the plain distribution.distribution_domain_name under *.cloudfront.net, a decision the module docstring says should be confirmed with the user before either gets added. The stack closes with eight cdk-nag suppressions, each with its own written reason: IAM4, IAM5, and L1 cover the framework-managed policies and Lambda that CDK's own BucketDeployment construct generates, S1 explains that a second bucket purely for access logging one static HTML page isn't worth the added infrastructure, CFR1 and CFR2 note that geo restriction and WAF are genuinely optional for an interview lab demo page, CFR3 makes the same access-logging tradeoff for CloudFront itself, and CFR4 notes that the distribution already sets TLS_V1_2_2021 in code and uses the default *.cloudfront.net certificate, which AWS enforces at TLS 1.2 or higher regardless, a case cdk-nag's check doesn't appear to recognize."],
  },
  {
    id: "blog-analytics",
    eyebrow: "Reading The Ledger",
    title: "The Visitor Ledger That Forgets on Purpose",
    illustration: "ledger.svg",
    diagram: "blog-analytics.svg",
    airport: ["A busy building keeps a visitor ledger, a simple record of who came through, when, and whether they made it all the way to the exhibit at the end of the hall. This project keeps one too, for this very blog post. It notes when someone arrives, whether they read to the end, whether they followed the link to the source archive on the way out, and roughly how long they stayed.", "The ledger is deliberately built to forget the fine grained detail after a while. Individual entries get shredded automatically after six months. What survives permanently is just the count, how many visitors, how many finished reading, how many followed the link, not who any specific one of them was. That is a real design choice, not an accident, and it is one worth being upfront about."],
    technicalTitle: "Blog Read Analytics",
    technical: ["The blog read analytics piece lives in its own stack, BlogAnalyticsStack, and it deploys unconditionally alongside the rest of the project the same way governance_stack.py does, because an API Gateway HTTP API, one low traffic Lambda function, and an on demand DynamoDB table add up to lab scale cents, not the kind of spend that needs a config.py feature flag to gate. Rather than standing up a second CloudFront distribution just to serve a beacon endpoint, it reaches into ThreeTierStack's existing distribution and adds a new cache behavior for the path pattern /analytics/*, the same cross stack construct mutation pattern auto_heal_stack.py already uses when it extends observability_stack.py's dashboard from a separate file. That behavior points at an origins.HttpOrigin built from the HTTP API's own domain, pulled out of the api_endpoint token with cdk.Fn.select(2, cdk.Fn.split(\"/\", self.http_api.api_endpoint)), and it runs with OriginProtocolPolicy.HTTPS_ONLY, ViewerProtocolPolicy.REDIRECT_TO_HTTPS, CachePolicy.CACHING_DISABLED, and OriginRequestPolicy.ALL_VIEWER_EXCEPT_HOST_HEADER. That combination means beacon calls are never cached, always travel encrypted, and arrive same origin from the browser's point of view, which is also why the API's own CORS configuration barely matters in production even though it is left wide open.", "On the client side, a component called AnalyticsBeacon.tsx posts JSON to that /analytics path, and the request lands on a single HttpApi named blog-analytics-api with exactly one route, POST /events, wired through an integration called IngestIntegration. The Lambda behind it, function name blog-analytics-ingest with the CDK construct id BlogAnalyticsIngest, runs Python 3.13 on the ARM_64 architecture with 256 MB of memory and a 10 second timeout, generous headroom for a function that does one DynamoDB put and one CloudWatch PutMetricData call per invocation and nothing else. Its entry point, handler.handler in handler.py, only accepts four event types, page_view, read_complete, github_click, and session_end, checked against an ALLOWED_EVENT_TYPES set, and anything outside that set gets a 400 response before it ever touches the table. A successful write returns a bare 204 with CORS headers attached, matching the fire and forget intent of a beacon, there is nothing for the client to read back. The stack also exposes the endpoint and the table name as CfnOutputs, BlogAnalyticsApiEndpoint and BlogAnalyticsTableName, so both are easy to find after a deploy without digging through the console.", "The table itself, construct id BlogAnalyticsEvents with the physical name blog-analytics-events, is a TableV2 keyed on sessionId as the partition key and eventTimestamp, a millisecond epoch number, as the sort key, so every event a single browser session generates lands as its own item under that session's partition. A global secondary index named EventTypeByTime reindexes the same data by eventType and eventTimestamp, so a dashboard query or any future ad hoc lookup can pull every page_view or session_end row across all sessions without a full table scan. Billing is on demand, encryption uses the customer managed key from security.buckets_key rather than an AWS owned key, point in time recovery is explicitly turned on, and the removal policy is DESTROY, consistent with every other lab scale resource in this project that the owner is comfortable losing on a stack teardown.", "The interesting design decision is what gets to live forever and what does not. Every item the handler writes carries an expiresAt attribute set to the current epoch second plus TTL_DAYS times 86400, and TTL_DAYS is 180, so every raw event, including the fields that actually matter for privacy, ages out of DynamoDB on its own after about six months with no cleanup job required. The source IP comes from the first entry in the X-Forwarded-For header, which CloudFront itself overwrites at the edge so a client cannot spoof it, and the country comes straight off the CloudFront-Viewer-Country header CloudFront injects on every request. Neither of those per visitor fields, sourceIp especially, is ever sent to CloudWatch. What the handler publishes under the LatticeLab/BlogAnalytics namespace is strictly aggregate: a count of 1 against PageViews, ReadCompletions, GithubClicks, or SessionEnds depending on event type, dimensioned only by Country, plus, for session_end events where timeOnPageMs is greater than zero, a TimeOnPageSeconds value and a MaxScrollPercent value with no dimension at all. CloudWatch metrics do not carry a TTL the way the DynamoDB items do, so the dashboard's reads and drop off panel and its engagement depth panel keep their full history long after the raw row behind any given data point has expired and disappeared. That split, raw and identifying data expires, aggregate and anonymous data persists, is exactly what the handler.py docstring argues for directly: IP addresses are personal data under GDPR and CCPA, so they are confined to the TTL'd raw events table and deliberately kept out of the permanent record.", "The ingest role, BlogAnalyticsIngestRole, is assumed only by lambda.amazonaws.com, bound by security.permissions_boundary like every other execution role in this project, and otherwise kept narrow apart from one necessary wildcard. It gets table.grant_write_data() for puts, security.buckets_key.grant_encrypt_decrypt() so it can write through the customer managed key, the AWS managed service-role/AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole policy for CloudWatch Logs, and a hand written statement allowing cloudwatch:PutMetricData against resources set to [\"*\"], because metric namespaces are not ARN scoped resources in IAM and there is no narrower way to grant that particular permission, the same reasoning governance_stack.py already used for its own GovernanceMetricsRole. The stack carries six matching cdk-nag suppressions: AwsSolutions-IAM4 for the managed logs policy, IAM5 for the PutMetricData wildcard, L1 because Python 3.13 is simply the newest managed Lambda runtime available at build time, APIG1 because access logging on a single route ingest API would just duplicate what the Lambda's own log group, BlogAnalyticsIngestLogGroup, one month retention, encrypted with security.logs_key, already captures, APIG4 because the endpoint is intentionally open write only telemetry with no per visitor identity to authorize against, and DDB3 because point in time recovery is already enabled on the table. Each suppression reads less like a shortcut and more like a paper trail, written into the code itself, explaining exactly why a relaxed rule is acceptable in this specific context."],
  },
  {
    id: "blog-assistant",
    eyebrow: "An Assistant Who Knows the Building",
    title: "The Guide Who Only Answers From the Building Plans",
    illustration: null,
    diagram: "blog-assistant.svg",
    airport: ["Imagine a guide stationed at the entrance who has actually read every plan, every manual, every record this airport has ever produced, and will answer any question about the place using only what is written in those documents. Ask something outside the documents and the guide says so honestly instead of making something up to sound helpful. Ask a follow up question and the guide remembers what was asked a moment ago, because the conversation gets written down as it happens, not just held in the guide's head where it disappears the moment the guide walks away.", "That is the assistant available on this very blog post. It answers questions about this project using the project's own writing, both the plain language sections and the technical sections, as its only source of truth. It keeps a short written record of each conversation so a follow up question makes sense in context, and that record gets cleared out automatically after thirty days rather than kept forever."],
    technicalTitle: "Retrieval Augmented Generation With Explicit Memory",
    technical: ["The assistant embedded on this blog is built as its own standalone stack, blog_assistant_stack.py, deployed unconditionally alongside blog_analytics_stack.py rather than gated behind the ENABLE_AI flag that controls agentic_ai_stack.py. The reasoning is simple. The blog itself is always on, so the thing answering questions about the blog needs to always be on too, with no feature flag standing between a visitor and a working chat box.", "Under the hood it mirrors agentic_ai_stack.py's semantic memory tier closely, because that tier already solved the hard problems once. An S3 Vectors vector bucket and index hold 1024 dimensional embeddings produced by Amazon Titan Embed Text v2, cosine distance, exactly the same configuration already proven in this project. A Bedrock Knowledge Base sits on top of that index, backed by an IAM role that needed the same two fixes the earlier build already discovered the hard way. S3 Vectors runs its own asynchronous indexing under its own service principal, indexing.s3vectors.amazonaws.com, which needs an explicit KMS grant separate from any general purpose grant on the bucket's encryption key, or indexing fails with an access denied error that has nothing obviously to do with the actual missing permission. And a plain role_arn reference on the Knowledge Base resource does not create a CloudFormation dependency on the separate IAM Policy resource that grants s3vectors:QueryVectors, so without an explicit iam.Policy construct held onto and referenced with add_dependency, the Knowledge Base can start creating before its own role actually has permission to query the index it needs, and fails with a permissions error that looks like a race condition because it is one.", "The content the knowledge base actually knows about is this project's own blog writing. Thirteen plain text files, one per section, each containing both the plain language explanation and the technical explanation for that layer, get uploaded to a private source bucket through a BucketDeployment at deploy time, and the Bedrock data source ingests them into the vector index from there. Nothing gets ingested per request. The knowledge base is built once, when the stack deploys, from whatever the blog's own content looked like at that moment.", "A single Lambda, blog-assistant-ask, handles every request end to end. It calls bedrock-agent-runtime Retrieve against the knowledge base to pull back the five most relevant passages for whatever the visitor just asked. It reads the conversation history for that visitor's conversation id from a dedicated S3 memory bucket, a plain JSON array of prior turns, capped at a fixed number of turns so the history cannot grow without bound. It builds a message list combining that history with the new question and the retrieved passages, and sends the whole thing to Anthropic's Claude model, config.BEDROCK_MODEL_ID, through Bedrock's Converse API, which is the modern, model family agnostic way to have a multi turn conversation with a Bedrock hosted model rather than hand building a model specific request body. Once the model responds, the handler appends both the question and the answer to the history and writes the updated array back to the same S3 object, then returns the answer to the caller.", "Getting a request there at all follows the same routing pattern blog_analytics_stack.py already established. CloudFront gets a new behavior, /assistant/star, added onto ThreeTierStack's existing distribution from inside this stack rather than inside threetier_stack.py itself, the same cross stack construct mutation pattern auto_heal_stack.py uses on observability_stack.py's dashboard. One detail worth being explicit about because it caused a real bug during this exact build: CloudFront forwards the full request path to the origin unchanged. A /assistant/star cache behavior does not strip that prefix before forwarding, so the API Gateway route registered in this stack has to be the full path the browser actually calls, /assistant/ask, not just /ask. Getting that wrong produces a working CloudFront distribution and a working API Gateway that still return a 404 to every real request, because the two sides are listening on different paths.", "Memory in this design is deliberately a plain S3 object rather than any framework provided session cache. That choice is consistent with how the rest of this project treats state. It can be opened, read, and deleted like any other file, not hidden behind an abstraction that only the framework itself can inspect."],
  },
  {
    id: "pipeline",
    eyebrow: "The Deployment Script",
    title: "The Construction Crew That Checks Its Own Permits First",
    illustration: "construction.svg",
    diagram: "pipeline.svg",
    airport: ["How does an airport get built and changed at all, without accidentally closing a live runway in the process? There is a construction crew whose blueprints live in a locked, version controlled records office, not a public bulletin board anyone can scribble on. Before that crew touches a single runway, they run through a fixed checklist: turn the blueprint into an actual build plan, and, this is the part most people miss, check whether their own permit approval process has changed since last time, and update that process first if it has.", "Only after that does the crew stop and wait for the airport director's physical signature. No signature, no groundbreaking, no matter how confident the crew is that the change is safe. That signature requirement exists specifically for anything with a real cost attached, and it never gets skipped."],
    technicalTitle: "The Deployment Pipeline",
    technical: ["The real, git driven deploy path runs through a single PipelineStack, and the first thing worth explaining about it is where the source lives. It reads from AWS CodeCommit, not GitHub, and the reason is written directly into the module docstring rather than left for someone to guess at later. The original plan was GitHub through CodeConnections, but that requires a GitHub App to be separately installed on the target repo, which is a different step from just OAuth authorizing the connection. Miss that install step and the Source stage fails with an error like \"No Branch [main] found,\" even though the connection itself shows AVAILABLE in the console, which makes it a genuinely easy trap to fall into and a frustrating one to debug from the CodePipeline side. CodeCommit sidesteps the whole handshake: the stack does a codecommit.Repository.from_repository_name lookup against config.CODECOMMIT_REPO_NAME, wraps it in pipelines.CodePipelineSource.code_commit against config.CODECOMMIT_BRANCH, and authenticates with plain IAM credentials. The CodeCommit repo itself is not created by this stack at all, it is provisioned out of band through the AWS CLI or console, which keeps the stack focused on the pipeline rather than repo bootstrapping. The pipeline construct itself is a pipelines.CodePipeline named \"Pipeline\" inside PipelineStack, given the friendly name \"lattice-landing-zone,\" with self_mutation=True, so a push that changes pipeline_stack.py itself causes the pipeline to redeploy its own definition before it ever gets to deploying application stacks.", "The Synth step is a pipelines.ShellStep named \"Synth\" and it does more than run cdk synth. The install_commands first run pyenv global 3.12.13, because the standard CodeBuild image manages multiple Python versions through pyenv and 3.12.13 is installed but not selected globally by default, so without that line python3.12 simply is not on PATH. From there it builds a virtual environment with python3.12 -m venv .venv, activates it, and installs from requirements.txt. The commands block is where the newer addition lives: before cdk synth ever runs, the build step cds into app/frontend-next and runs npm ci followed by npm run build, then returns to the repo root. This exists because ThreeTierStack's web tier deploys the static export output of that Next.js app (built with Next's output: 'export' setting), and CDK's Source.asset() call needs real files on disk at synth time, not a promise that they will exist later. Node and npm are already present on the standard CodeBuild image since CDK itself depends on them, so no extra install step was needed to make that work. Only after the frontend build finishes does the step run npx cdk synth. The synth step also carries an env block setting ENABLE_CLOUDWAN, ENABLE_AI, and ENABLE_SAGEMAKER all to \"true,\" and that block is flagged in a comment as deliberately temporary: Cloud WAN's core network and attachments, the Bedrock AgentCore and S3 Vectors and Knowledge Base pieces, and the SageMaker Async Inference endpoint all cost real money layered on top of what the rest of the project already runs, and the comment is explicit that these should be reverted to unset or explicitly \"false\" once a given demo is done, before the next pipeline run picks them back up.", "Nothing in LandingZoneStage actually deploys without a human saying so first. pipeline.add_stage is called with pre=[pipelines.ManualApprovalStep(\"PromoteToLandingZone\")], so the stage sits behind an approval action in the CodePipeline console and a reviewer has to click approve before CloudFormation touches a single resource in the stage. There is a smaller but equally deliberate piece of plumbing right after that: pipeline.build_pipeline() is called explicitly, which forces the underlying CodeBuild synth project and its IAM role to materialize immediately rather than being resolved lazily during CDK's own synth pass, and it has to run after add_stage or the LandingZone stage and its approval step would be locked out of a pipeline structure that had already been finalized. Once the project exists, the stack grants its role an iam.PolicyStatement allowing ec2:DescribeAvailabilityZones against all resources. That grant exists because cdk.context.json is deliberately not committed to the repo, since it is an account specific build artifact that has no business sitting in a public repo. Without a cached context file, every synth, including this one running fresh inside CodeBuild with no local cache, has to resolve each ec2.Vpc construct's availability zones through a live describe call instead of a cached lookup, and the read only, account wide grant is treated as a smaller and more honest footprint than checking in a file that goes stale and is tied to one AWS account anyway.", "Once approved, the stage deploys in an order that is not arbitrary. SecurityStack goes first, full stop, because every other stack in the stage takes its CMKs and its permissions boundary in as constructor props rather than looking them up again later, which stage.py's own docstring calls out directly as \"not by re-lookup.\" NetworkStack comes next, standing up the VPCs and Transit Gateway that later stacks build against. InspectionStack, PrivateLinkStack, and ThreeTierStack all follow, each one taking network and security as constructor props so they can build their GWLB endpoints, VPC endpoints, and application resources against the VPCs that already exist rather than trying to discover them. ThreeTierStack carries an explicit threetier.add_dependency(privatelink) that is not a functional dependency at all, it is there to fix a race. Both stacks add a small EC2 instance, LatticeInstanceTargetHost from the Lattice side and ProviderInstance from any future EC2 rollback path, and both draw against the same account level vCPU quota. Deploying them in the same parallel wave meant that whichever stack was mid replacement, freeing up vCPU as it went, had not always finished before the other one tried to claim that same capacity, so CloudFormation would fail with \"requested more vCPU capacity than your current vCPU limit\" even though the headroom genuinely existed a few seconds later. Forcing PrivateLinkStack to finish first removes that race outright rather than leaning on retry timing to paper over it. LatticeStack comes after, taking network, threetier, and security as props, followed by ResourceGroupsStack and DiagramStack.", "The rest of the order follows the same logic of letting real dependencies dictate sequencing rather than guessing. GovernanceStack and DriftRemediationStack deploy next, unconditionally, since CloudTrail, Config, SecurityHub, and GuardDuty at this scale cost cents rather than the dollars that gate the optional layers further down. ObservabilityStack now deploys after both of those, a change from an earlier layout where it sat right after LatticeStack, because its dashboard graphs governance_stack.py's custom metrics and drift_remediation_stack.py's Lambda directly, and a dashboard cannot graph a metric that does not exist yet. BlogAnalyticsStack comes after that, waiting on both ThreeTierStack, since it adds a new CloudFront behavior onto ThreeTierStack's existing distribution, and ObservabilityStack, since it adds widgets onto that stack's existing dashboard. KafkaStack is conditional on config.ENABLE_KAFKA. The Cloud WAN pair is more involved: CloudWanStack deploys with cross_region_references=True and its own explicit environment in config.AWS_REGION, and Region2Stack deploys separately into config.SECOND_REGION (us east 2), consuming cloudwan.core_network.attr_core_network_id across that region boundary, which is exactly what cross_region_references exists to allow. SageMakerStack is gated behind config.ENABLE_SAGEMAKER. AgenticAiStack deploys last among the optional layers, after network hardening, governance, Cloud WAN, and SageMaker, because its MCP tools read state from all of them, and it only wires in the cloudwan_topology and detect_anomalies tools when the cloudwan and sagemaker objects it was handed are not None. AutoHealStack closes the whole stage out, deployed unconditionally and last, specifically because it sits downstream of every optional layer that may or may not exist by the time it runs.", "One last wrinkle worth naming is how app.py and stage.py relate to each other, since it explains why the pipeline and local iteration do not collide. app.py instantiates LandingZoneStage twice, once directly as \"LandingZone-Dev\" for fast local work with cdk deploy --all, and once implicitly inside PipelineStack, which builds its own separate LandingZoneStage named plainly \"LandingZone.\" That duplication is not an accident or leftover code, it is required, because a CDK Stage can only be parented once, so the pipeline cannot reuse the Dev instance even if it wanted to. cdk-nag's AwsSolutionsChecks is applied only to the Dev stage for the same reason cited in app.py's own comment, checking the pipeline's internal CodeBuild and CodePipeline plumbing, or its nested copy of an identical stage, would just double the findings to review without adding real coverage. It is a small detail, but it is the kind of detail that tells you the pipeline was built by someone who had actually hit the Stage-can-only-be-parented-once error once already."],
  },
  {
    id: "cloudwan",
    eyebrow: "Growing Beyond One Property",
    title: "Joining an Airline Alliance",
    illustration: "alliance.svg",
    diagram: "cloudwan.svg",
    airport: ["Say this airport wants to start codesharing flights with a sister airport in another city. Ripping out the existing taxiway system to do it would ground every flight already scheduled, so instead a new, purpose built alliance network gets stood up alongside the old one, and traffic migrates onto it a little at a time. The existing taxiway system stays running the whole time, registered with the new alliance network as a bridge rather than replaced outright.", "It is worth saying plainly that one part of this migration did not go perfectly. A specific route into one segment of the new network could not be fully completed during this build, a genuine limitation on the provider's side rather than something this project got wrong. Rather than hide that, the gap is documented directly in the code where anyone reviewing it will actually see it. This whole alliance layer only turns on when explicitly requested, because a second city is not free to operate."],
    technicalTitle: "Multi Region Cloud WAN",
    technical: ["The backbone of this section is cloudwan_stack.py, which stands up an AWS Cloud WAN core network as policy-as-code rather than click-ops. The stack creates an nm.CfnGlobalNetwork construct named GlobalNetwork, then an nm.CfnCoreNetwork named CoreNetwork that hangs off it, and every routing decision the core network makes lives in a single Python dictionary called POLICY_DOCUMENT that gets passed straight into policy_document. That matters because Cloud WAN's entire behavior, which VPCs land in which segment, which segments can talk to which others, is expressed as versioned code sitting in the repo instead of state that only exists in the console. The core network id comes back out as an SSM parameter at /lattice-lab/cloudwan/core-network-id, which is how the cross-region stack finds it later without any manual copy-pasting of ARNs between accounts or regions.", "POLICY_DOCUMENT defines four segments, and the names were chosen to mirror the customer's own vocabulary rather than generic AWS terms: FastTrack for production, SkyPath as the proxy segment, SkyTransit for hybrid and on-prem reachability, and Workload. Workload is the interesting one because it sets isolate-attachments to true, meaning nothing inside that segment can reach anything else inside that segment unless you explicitly grant it through a share action, which is the segment-isolation half of the demo. SkyTransit is the opposite case, it gets a segment-actions entry with action set to share, mode set to attachment-route, sharing itself into FastTrack, which is the controlled cross-segment reachability half. Attachments get sorted into these segments automatically through attachment-policies, four rules numbered 100, 110, 120, and 130, each matching on a tag-value condition against a key of segment and routing constant-association style into FastTrack, SkyPath, SkyTransit, or Workload respectively. In practice that means app_vpc gets tagged segment equals WORKLOAD and provider_vpc gets tagged segment equals FASTTRACK on their CfnVpcAttachment resources, and the policy engine does the rest, the stack itself never manually wires an attachment into a segment.", "The cross-region piece lives in region2_stack.py, which only deploys when config.ENABLE_CLOUDWAN is on and stage.py has set env to us-east-2 with cross_region_references set to True. It builds one small VPC, Region2ProdVpc with vpc_name region2-prod-vpc, sized from config.REGION2_VPC_CIDRS under the region2_prod key, capped at a single AZ, zero NAT gateways, and PRIVATE_ISOLATED subnets only at a /24 mask. There is no internet gateway and no NAT anywhere in this VPC, the only way in is Session Manager over interface endpoints for SSM, SSM_MESSAGES, and EC2_MESSAGES, gated by a security group that only allows inbound HTTPS on port 443 from the VPC's own CIDR block. That is the same SSM-only, no-bastion pattern used everywhere else in the project, just replicated into a second region. The test instance itself runs on config.DEFAULT_INSTANCE_TYPE with the latest Amazon Linux 2023 AMI, an IAM role carrying AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore, ssm_session_permissions turned on, and an 8 GB encrypted EBS root volume. Its CfnVpcAttachment, named Region2VpcAttachment, is tagged segment equals WORKLOAD and passed the core_network_id that was threaded in from the us-east-1 stack through that SSM parameter, so the attachment falls into the same isolated Workload segment as app_vpc back in the home region, purely through the tag-driven policy rather than any region-specific wiring.", "One honest wrinkle shows up in that same file: security_stack.py's customer-managed KMS keys are regional to us-east-1, and KMS keys do not span regions without setting up an explicit multi-region key, which was out of scope for this lab. So the region2 EBS volume falls back to the AWS-managed default key instead of the project's usual CMK-everywhere pattern. That is called out directly in the module docstring as a deliberate, documented exception rather than something that slipped through review.", "The core network's edge locations carry their own ASNs, us-east-1 at 64513 and config.SECOND_REGION at 64514, drawn from an asn-ranges block of 64513 to 64555. That starting number is not arbitrary. The project's existing Transit Gateway, built in network_stack.py, was left to take AWS's default AmazonSideAsn, which landed on 64512, the bottom of the standard 16-bit private ASN block AWS hands out automatically. The first pass at this policy reused that same 64512 range for the edge location's own ASN and it caused a real BGP ASN collision, confirmed live through direct networkmanager create-transit-gateway-peering calls against the actual TGW, which failed the same way whether the registration was freshly created or fully propagated, which ruled out timing as the cause. Each side of a TGW-to-Core-Network peering needs a distinct ASN the same way any BGP session does, so the fix was to start the core network's asn-ranges one number above the TGW's own ASN, at 64513, so the two can never collide again even if the TGW's default-assigned ASN ever shifts within its own range.", "The registration and peering between that existing TGW and the new core network both work, and work reliably. CfnTransitGatewayRegistration (TgwRegistration) and CfnTransitGatewayPeering (TgwPeering) both reach AVAILABLE consistently, confirmed live. What does not work, and is documented as such rather than quietly left out, is the next step in a real TGW-to-Cloud-WAN migration: attaching one of the TGW's route tables to a segment so that routes actually exchange between the two. The CfnTransitGatewayRouteTableAttachment resource type consistently fails with an opaque Fail to create Resource error carrying HandlerErrorCode InvalidRequest and no further detail in the reason string. This was chased down across more than six attempts covering every plausible root cause: the ASN collision described above, which was real and got fixed, a live route table versus a dedicated one, tested both, identical failure even against a freshly created, verified-empty, unassociated table, registration and peering propagation timing, both confirmed AVAILABLE well before each attempt, and the peering's own underlying EC2 transit gateway attachment, confirmed available. None of those explain the failure, and the API itself gives nothing else to go on. So the honest state of this project is that the TGW is genuinely integrated with the core network at the registration and peering layer, but the route-table-level route-sharing half of a full TGW migration is not demonstrated here, and that gap is called out in the stack's own docstring rather than papered over."],
  },
  {
    id: "agentic-ai",
    eyebrow: "A Co-Pilot, Not a Pilot",
    title: "The Assistant in the Tower Who Cannot Touch the Yoke",
    illustration: "copilot.svg",
    diagram: "agentic-ai.svg",
    airport: ["Put an assistant in the control tower who can see everything a human controller sees, every sensor, every camera feed, every log from the security operations center, and who can actually answer real questions about what is happening. Ask why a gate is backed up and it will go look and tell you. Ask it to draft a proposed fix and it will.", "But it cannot move a single plane. When it has a genuine suggestion, it writes up a formal change request and puts it in a human supervisor's inbox, exactly the way a junior controller escalates an idea to their shift lead rather than acting on it directly. The assistant has broad read access to the whole airport and almost no write access to anything that matters, and the one narrow exception still ends with a human reviewing the request before anything changes."],
    technicalTitle: "The Agentic AI Layer",
    technical: ["The agentic AI layer lives in stacks/agentic_ai_stack.py and is gated behind config.ENABLE_AI, off by default, because Bedrock AgentCore plus S3 Vectors plus a Knowledge Base are all meaningfully billable even sitting idle. The stack stands up two agent personas, network-operator and connectivity-planner, each with its own IAM role, its own route on a shared HTTP API (api_name agentic-ai-api), and its own Bedrock AgentCore Runtime, all pointed at the same orchestrator Lambda code (orchestrator/handler.py, a Bedrock Converse tool-use loop) but scoped to a different tool set through a TOOL_FUNCTION_ARNS environment variable built per persona. network-operator gets every read-only tool. connectivity-planner gets everything network-operator has plus one additional tool. The separation is enforced in IAM, not in a system prompt: the loop that grants tool_fns[tool_name].grant_invoke(orch_role) only iterates over that persona's own allowed_tools list, so network-operator's Lambda execution role has no invoke permission on the write-capable tool at all, full stop.", "Memory is deliberately three tiers instead of one DynamoDB table. Working memory is a DynamoDB TableV2 named agentic-ai-working-memory, partition key session_id, on-demand billing, encrypted with the project's customer-managed key (security.secrets_key), point-in-time recovery on, and a ttl attribute so idle sessions expire themselves instead of accumulating forever. Durable memory is a plain S3 bucket (DurableMemoryBucket), versioned, KMS encrypted with security.buckets_key, that holds an append-only JSONL transcript per session and is kept indefinitely, independent of DynamoDB's TTL, until someone runs cdk destroy. Semantic memory is the deep one: an S3 Vectors bucket named agentic-ai-semantic-memory backed by a CfnIndex called semantic-memory-index (float32 vectors, 1024 dimensions, cosine distance), fronted by a Bedrock Knowledge Base of the same name that embeds ingested content with amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0. That's the tier the search_memory MCP tool actually queries at runtime, via bedrock-agent-runtime:Retrieve scoped to the Knowledge Base's own ARN. Getting the semantic tier live surfaced two real permission gaps worth naming: S3 Vectors' async indexing runs under its own service identity (indexing.s3vectors.amazonaws.com), which needed an explicit KMS encrypt/decrypt grant separate from any bucket-level grant, confirmed by a live CREATE_FAILED with an AccessDenied on asynchronous indexing until that grant was added; and the Knowledge Base's IAM role needed its policy attached as an explicit iam.Policy construct with an add_dependency, because role_arn alone doesn't imply CloudFormation waits for a separately-attached policy, which produced a real race (CREATE_FAILED, no identity-based policy allows s3vectors:QueryVectors) on the first two attempts.", "Seven MCP tool Lambdas back the actual tool surface: query_kafka, search_memory, get_network_health, query_governance, cloudwan_topology, detect_anomalies, and propose_connectivity. Every one is PYTHON_3_13 on ARM_64, VPC-attached into the private subnets behind a shared security group (McpToolSg, outbound only), with its own least-privilege IAM role built by the same _tool_role helper. query_kafka calls vpc-lattice:GetResourceConfiguration and vpc-lattice:GetResourceGateway against the Lattice resource-gateway attrs; get_network_health calls elasticloadbalancing:DescribeTargetHealth and ec2:DescribeVpnConnections; query_governance calls securityhub:GetFindings and config:DescribeComplianceByConfigRule; cloudwan_topology (only deployed when the Cloud WAN layer is enabled) calls networkmanager:GetCoreNetwork and ListAttachments; detect_anomalies queries a DynamoDB table that only really exists once the SageMaker stack deploys, degrading cleanly to an empty env var otherwise. Each of these seven is registered twice: once as a direct lambda:InvokeFunction target the orchestrator Lambda calls straight from Python (cheaper than round-tripping through MCP from inside another Lambda), and once as a genuine Bedrock AgentCore CfnGatewayTarget on a CfnGateway named agentic-ai-mcp-gateway (protocol_type MCP, authorizer_type AWS_IAM), so any real external MCP client, the AgentCore console, a different agent framework, a human with an MCP inspector, can discover and call the same tool over the actual MCP protocol. Wiring the Gateway targets up exposed a third gotcha worth naming plainly: granting the gateway's execution role invoke permission at the IAM identity level (fn.grant_invoke(gateway_role)) was not sufficient on its own, every GatewayTarget failed to create with an error that the gateway execution role lacked permission to invoke the Lambda. What actually fixed it was adding a resource-based policy directly on each Lambda, an explicit lambda_.CfnPermission with the gateway role's ARN as principal, on top of the identity-based grant, matching what AWS's own docs show for Lambda-backed Gateway targets.", "propose_connectivity is the one tool in the whole layer with any write capability, and its blast radius is intentionally tiny. Its IAM role is allowed exactly four CodeCommit actions, GetBranch, CreateBranch, CreateCommit, and CreatePullRequest, scoped to the single repository named in config.CODECOMMIT_REPO_NAME, and nothing else. It never touches a running resource. It drafts a connectivity change and opens a pull request against the base branch in config.CODECOMMIT_BRANCH for a human to review and merge, which is also why it gets a longer 60 second timeout where every other tool Lambda defaults to 30. Only connectivity-planner's persona list includes it (PLANNER_ONLY_TOOLS), so only that persona's orchestrator role ever receives grant_invoke on it. network-operator physically cannot reach it, at the IAM level, regardless of what an operator asks the model to do, which is the point of the whole exercise: nowhere in this stack does either persona's IAM policy grant a single infrastructure-mutating action against real network resources.", "The Bedrock AgentCore primitives themselves show up as five distinct CloudFormation resource types, each used for what it's actually for rather than as decoration. CfnMemory (named agentic_ai_memory) is AgentCore's own native session-memory service, configured with a semantic_default strategy and a summary_default strategy, indexed on session_id and agent_role, a 30 day event expiry, and encrypted with the same secrets_key CMK as the working-memory table; it's explicitly complementary to, not a replacement for, the three custom tiers described above. CfnWorkloadIdentity (named agentic-ai-lab) backs the Gateway's workload identity. CfnRuntime is provisioned twice, once per persona (agentic_ai_network_operator and agentic_ai_connectivity_planner), against a stub S3 code asset (agent_runtime_stub/agent_runtime.py), protocol_configuration HTTP, network_configuration PUBLIC, because AgentCore Runtime's real invocation contract is an always-on HTTP server, a genuinely different shape than the Lambda handler contract the actual working demo uses. That's called out honestly in the module docstring as a stub provisioned to demonstrate the construct end to end, the same pattern the project already uses for its Kafka stack. The real working path a caller actually hits is the Cognito-protected one: an HttpApi named agentic-ai-api with an HttpJwtAuthorizer backed by a Cognito user pool (agentic-ai-api-users, self sign up disabled, minimum password length 12 with upper, lower, digit and symbol required) sitting in front of both /network-operator and /connectivity-planner routes, because an HTTP API's default execute-api domain is public internet by construction and there's no VPC Link path for a Lambda-proxy integration, so leaving those routes unauthenticated would have been the one clearly un-zero-trust decision in an otherwise IAM-scoped design."],
  },
  {
    id: "sagemaker",
    eyebrow: "Listening for Trouble",
    title: "The Seismograph Under the Runway",
    illustration: "seismograph.svg",
    diagram: "sagemaker.svg",
    airport: ["Sensors under the runway log every vibration, day after day, building up a picture of what normal actually looks like. Once a week that history trains a fresh model of normal. Every six hours, new readings get compared against that model in bulk, and anything that does not fit gets flagged, not because a person wrote a rule for it, but because it deviates from the pattern the model learned on its own.", "Findings get handed straight to the assistant in the tower, who can factor them into whatever question gets asked next. And because the actual training and scoring jobs are not the kind of thing that can just be torn down cleanly like an ordinary resource, a dedicated cleanup routine sweeps up everything those jobs created whenever this whole layer gets shut off, so nothing keeps quietly running (and billing) after the fact."],
    technicalTitle: "The SageMaker Anomaly Pipeline",
    technical: ["The starting fact that shapes this entire stack is that AWS::SageMaker::TrainingJob and AWS::SageMaker::TransformJob simply do not exist as CloudFormation resource types. A training job and a transform job are one shot API calls, not declarative infrastructure, so there is nothing for CDK to own in the usual sense. Everything downstream of that fact in sagemaker_stack.py is boto3 driven from inside Lambda functions rather than synthesized as CfnResources, and the stack's docstring is explicit about why: there is no model artifact to point CreateModel at until an actual training run has produced one. So the pipeline is six pieces stitched together with EventBridge rules and Lambda, not a static template.", "Data starts with an ec2.FlowLog named AppVpcFlowLog attached directly to app_vpc, writing into the raw/ prefix of a single bucket called SageMakerDataBucket. That bucket is deliberately not split into four or five buckets, it is one KMS encrypted, versioned, block-all-public-access bucket partitioned by prefix (raw/, processed/, training-output/, transform-output/, findings/, async-output/), with a lifecycle rule that expires anything under raw/ after 30 days so the flow log firehose does not grow unbounded. A daily Lambda called flow_log_preprocessor (function name rcf-flowlog-flow-log-preprocessor, five minute timeout at 300 seconds) reads that raw prefix and writes numeric feature CSVs into processed/. Once a week, on a seven day EventBridge rate schedule, rcf_trainer calls CreateTrainingJob against that processed data using the built in Random Cut Forest algorithm container. The image URI is built at synth time from the constant RCF_TRAINING_IMAGE_ACCOUNT, 382416733822, which the code comments note is AWS's documented built in algorithm registry account, templated per region as {account}.dkr.ecr.{region}.amazonaws.com/randomcutforest:latest rather than hardcoded to one region.", "Training completion fires an EventBridge rule called TrainingJobCompletionRule, matching source aws.sagemaker, detail type SageMaker Training Job State Change, and a TrainingJobName prefix of rcf-flowlog-training-, which invokes rcf_model_promoter. On the very first successful run this Lambda creates the SageMaker model, endpoint config, and the endpoint itself, named rcf-flowlog-endpoint. On every run after that it updates the existing endpoint in place instead, then deletes the previous generation's model and endpoint config so old generations do not pile up as orphaned resources. The endpoint type is Async Inference, and the stack's opening comment is unusually direct about why that choice was made over Serverless Inference: Serverless Inference endpoints have no vpc_config option at all, which would mean scoring live VPC Flow Log data from an endpoint sitting entirely outside app-vpc's network boundary, a real break in the project's zero trust posture. Async Inference supports VpcConfig and supports scaling its instance count to zero through Application Auto Scaling when idle, which the promoter registers on first creation via RegisterScalableTarget and PutScalingPolicy with MAX_CAPACITY set to 1, meaning the endpoint scales between zero and one instance rather than sitting warm around the clock.", "Every six hours, on an events.Schedule.rate(Duration.hours(6)) rule, rcf_batch_scorer calls CreateTransformJob against the same processed/ data using whatever model generation is currently promoted to the endpoint. This is a deliberately separate, systematic bulk sweep, distinct from any on demand invocation path against the live endpoint. When that transform job completes, a second rule, TransformJobCompletionRule, matches detail type SageMaker Transform Job State Change with a TransformJobName prefix of rcf-batch-score- and hands off to rcf_findings_processor. That Lambda reads the RCF anomaly scores, keeps anything at or above an ANOMALY_SCORE_THRESHOLD of 3.0, and writes qualifying findings into a DynamoDB table named sagemaker-anomaly-findings (partition key finding_type as a string, sort key detected_at as a number, on demand billing, customer managed KMS encryption via security.secrets_key, point in time recovery turned on) as well as into S3 and out to an SNS topic also named sagemaker-anomaly-findings, which carries an email subscription to config.REMEDIATION_EMAIL. That same DynamoDB table is what agentic_ai_stack.py's detect_anomalies MCP tool reads from, which is the actual point of the whole pipeline: turning raw flow log noise into something an agent can query.", "Because the endpoint, its model, its endpoint config, and its Application Auto Scaling target are all created imperatively at Lambda runtime rather than declared at synth time, cdk destroy has nothing native to clean up when the stack is torn down, which is exactly the gap TeardownCleanup exists to close. It is wired in as a Custom Resource, a cr.Provider wrapping the cleanup Lambda as its on_event_handler, so it fires specifically on stack deletion. Its role is granted ListEndpoints, DeleteEndpoint, ListEndpointConfigs, DeleteEndpointConfig, ListModels, DeleteModel, and DeregisterScalableTarget, all scoped to resource '*' because there is no ARN to narrow to in advance, and the real safety net is the NAME_PREFIX convention, rcf-flowlog-, that every resource this pipeline creates at runtime follows, so the sweep only touches what this pipeline actually owns. The teardown Lambda itself runs with a 120 second timeout and, notably, is never given an explicit function_name the way the five orchestrator Lambdas are, since it does not need to be discoverable by the NAME_PREFIX convention, it is the one doing the discovering.", "The networking around all of this stays consistent with the rest of the project's zero trust stance. Two interface VPC endpoints, SAGEMAKER_API for control plane calls like CreateTrainingJob and SAGEMAKER_RUNTIME for InvokeEndpointAsync on the data plane, sit in the Private subnet group behind a security group that only allows inbound tcp 443 from the app-vpc's own CIDR block. They are created sequentially rather than in parallel, with an explicit node dependency between them, to sidestep the same Route53 private hosted zone provisioning race the project had already run into with the paired Bedrock endpoints in agentic_ai_stack.py. The orchestrator Lambdas' own security group, SageMakerLambdaSg, is outbound only with no ingress rules at all, which is all five of them need since nothing calls into a Lambda ENI directly. The stack closes with four cdk-nag suppressions on its own IAM choices (IAM4 for the AWS managed VPC access and basic execution policies, IAM5 for the wildcard resource on sagemaker and application-autoscaling actions, justified by the same NAME_PREFIX reasoning as the teardown sweep, L1 for Python 3.13 being the current runtime, and S1 for the data bucket's access logs pointing at a logs bucket that deliberately does not log to itself), which is a small but telling sign that the wildcards here were a considered tradeoff rather than an oversight."],
  },
  {
    id: "kafka",
    eyebrow: "Planned, Not Yet Built",
    title: "The Wing Still on the Blueprint",
    illustration: "blueprint.svg",
    diagram: null,
    airport: ["Not every part of an airport gets built in the first phase, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest. There is a wing on the blueprint that has not been built yet, meant eventually to house a message broker that other parts of the airport will eventually rely on for passing information around in real time. It is marked clearly as future work, feature flagged off, sitting in the plans on purpose rather than quietly missing.", "Including a deliberately unfinished piece in a portfolio project is its own kind of honesty. It shows how a real plan gets staged over time, not just what a finished result looks like at the end."],
    technicalTitle: "The Planned Kafka Layer",
    technical: ["stacks/kafka_stack.py is the one file in this project I am not going to pretend is finished. The class is called KafkaStack, it subclasses the standard aws_cdk Stack, and its own docstring says exactly what it is: \"real KRaft Kafka on EC2, deployed last, feature-flagged off first.\" It points directly at SPEC.md Section 6, which laid out the same instruction before a line of stack code existed. Whether this stack even gets instantiated is controlled entirely from the outside, by config.ENABLE_KAFKA in stage.py. The class itself has no internal flag check, and that is called out explicitly in the module docstring, because the gating belongs at the composition layer where all the stacks get wired together, not scattered inside each individual construct.", "Right now the constructor does almost nothing. It calls super().__init__, and it tags itself with Tags.of(self).add(\"Layer\", config.Layer.KAFKA), which is the same tagging convention every other stack in this project follows so that cost and resource ownership can be traced back to a layer at a glance. After that there is a block of TODO comments and nothing else. No EC2 instance, no security group, no VPC lookup, no CfnOutput. It is, in the most literal sense, a skeleton: a stack class that exists, is wired into the app, and today produces an empty CloudFormation template.", "What it is meant to become is spelled out in those TODO comments, and I want to be precise about it rather than paraphrase loosely, because the whole point of this section is honesty about what is built versus what is planned. The plan is a self-managed Kafka broker running in KRaft mode, meaning no ZooKeeper, on an EC2 instance placed inside onprem-vpc. User data on that instance would install Kafka, format its storage with kafka-storage.sh, and start the broker listening on port 9092, with the advertised listener set to the instance's own private IP so that clients on the other side of the mesh resolve back to something actually reachable instead of a loopback address. Once that broker is running, the plan calls for repointing LatticeStack's L4e resource configuration at it, which means swapping out the placeholder ncat listener that NetworkStack currently exposes and replacing it with the real broker endpoint. The last item in the TODO list is a producer and consumer test that has to be reachable from the app-vpc consumer through the Lattice mesh itself, which is really the whole thesis of the project: prove that a service in one VPC can talk to a workload in a completely separate VPC entirely through Lattice's service network, no VPC peering, no transit gateway route table entries.", "I included this stack unfinished on purpose, and I think that is worth saying directly rather than letting a reviewer wonder why an empty class is sitting in the repo. Deploying self-managed Kafka on EC2 is a meaningfully bigger and riskier step than anything else in this project so far, it means an always-on instance, storage that has to be provisioned and formatted correctly the first time, and a broker configuration that has real consequences if the advertised listener is wrong. Rather than fake that work with a stack that looks complete but was never actually exercised against a real broker, I staged it: the class exists, the tagging convention is already correct, the deploy order is already decided (last, after everything else is stable), and the feature flag that will turn it on lives in exactly the same place as every other environment toggle in config.py. Anyone reading this code six months from now, including me, can see precisely what is left to do without having to reconstruct intent from a commit message.", "The honest reason I have not built it out yet also matters, and it is not laziness. This project has been running against a tight vCPU quota, and adding an always-on EC2 broker competes directly with instance capacity I do not currently have headroom for. Rather than force it in and destabilize a working Lattice mesh, I left KafkaStack exactly where SPEC.md Section 6 says it should sit until that constraint clears: staged, flagged off, and ready to be finished in the order the plan already specifies. That is the part I actually want a hiring panel to notice, not that every stack in this repository is done, but that the ones that are not done are marked as such deliberately, sequenced correctly, and gated behind a flag instead of being quietly hidden or, worse, half wired into stacks that depend on them."],
  },
];